/* Shared, authored documentation. Never put catalog, model, or user HTML here. */
window.SpectraScopeManual = Object.freeze({
  edition: 'Geo Intelligence application guide · 9 November 2026',
  chapters: [
    {id:'hls-bundles',group:'Data and catalog',title:'Build a three-date HLS bundle',summary:'Search NASA HLS and prepare six reflectance bands per date for Prithvi Crop/LULC.',keywords:'HLS L30 S30 Earthdata Landsat Sentinel-2 Prithvi Maxar CBERS',html:'<ol><li>Search HLS L30 or HLS S30 for your area and dates. Use NASA Earthdata Login to download the science files. You can request several dates in one Earthdata batch.</li><li>Import the folder. The catalog identifies each HLS tile and date, preserves its science bands and lists quality and angle files separately.</li><li>In Local imagery, select three distinct dates from the same HLS tile and choose <b>Build 3-date HLS bundle</b>. The app verifies the common 30 m grid and chooses blue, green, red, near infrared and both shortwave infrared bands from each date.</li><li>Add the bundle to a mission and choose Prithvi Crop/LULC in Manual inference. The original acquisitions remain in the catalog.</li></ol><p>CBERS-4A WPM Level 4 is searchable through INPE. For Maxar WorldView or GeoEye imagery from the <a href="https://registry.opendata.aws/maxar-open-data/">Maxar event archive</a>, download from the provider and import locally. Select Maxar in the band inspector if the source metadata does not identify it.</p>'},
    {id:'three-workspaces',group:'Start here',title:'The three mission workspaces',summary:'Automatic pipelines, Training & qualification, and Manual inference.',keywords:'workflow automatic training qualification release pretrained manual inference pause stop logs',html:'<p>Open a mission and choose one of its three workspaces. The catalog, source data, review desk, layers and Insights support these workspaces.</p><ol><li><b>Automatic pipelines:</b> choose an AOI, sensors and a local or online catalog. Connect installed algorithms and export blocks in the diagram. Validate and save the diagram, then activate monitoring explicitly. The pipeline discovers intersecting images and tracks each execution.</li><li><b>Training &amp; qualification:</b> define the classes for a locally trainable model. Label or import reference data, assigning independent validation regions separately. Check sample coverage, train a candidate, and review Precision, Recall and F1 against your goals. Sample counts are guidance, not measured accuracy. Confirm your review, add a release note and use the qualification action to make the version available in Manual inference and pipelines. Returning it to Training blocks its operational use.</li><li><b>Manual inference:</b> select images and one or more compatible models. Native pretrained models are available directly, without local training. SAM requires source-image prompts. Review results explicitly and choose the export format.</li></ol><p>Pause, Resume and Stop act at safe processing checkpoints; they may wait for the current native operation. Stop preserves existing inputs and artifacts. Deletion archives operational records rather than erasing scientific evidence. Use the mission logs to inspect errors and download diagnostics.</p><p>Object detectors require suitable image resolution; Sentinel/Landsat cannot resolve ordinary small vehicles reliably. Published benchmark results are not local accuracy. Open <b>Data &amp; results → Services &amp; workers</b> for private source connections, coordinate conversions, algorithm inspection and worker settings.</p>'},
    {id:'discovery-drafts',group:'Data and catalog',title:'Find or connect a read-only source',summary:'Search approved folders and saved sources, or prepare an inspection or connection.',keywords:'agent discovery command API OGC Features catalog local chat read-only triangulation',html:`<p>Open <b>Services &amp; workers</b>, then <b>Describe a discovery request</b>. Enter <code>find catalog archive</code> or <code>find triangulation camera</code>, then choose <b>Find or prepare</b>. The search checks saved configurations and files directly inside approved folders. It uses filenames, not file contents. Approve the specific folder if the file is in a subfolder.</p><p>With local Ollama configured, select <b>Use local chat to interpret the request</b> to ask in ordinary language. The local model chooses a search type and optional name. It cannot contact suppliers, connect to results or scan the network.</p><p>If you know the source, enter <code>inspect catalog /data/catalog.sqlite</code>, <code>connect ogc_features https://internal-host/features</code>, or <code>connect mcp 10.0.0.2:9443/mcp</code>. The form opens for review. Add credentials only in the connection form. For OGC Features, map the local raster-path property and optionally the top-level identifier and geometry. Select rows, review the import plan and approve indexing.</p><p>For MCP, choose a listed GeoJSON imagery resource in <b>API connections</b> and read it. Map the image path and optional ID and footprint, then review the import plan. The app uses the saved snapshot for preview and indexing, and opens selected local raster paths only inside approved source roots.</p><p>An OpenAPI inventory can suggest an existing STAC or OGC connector. Preparing it fills a draft. Review the endpoint and choose Connect read-only to run the verified connector checks. Documented operations and server URLs are never used to connect automatically.</p><p>For ArcGIS Enterprise 11.x, select <b>ArcGIS Enterprise FeatureServer layer</b> and enter the full internal HTTPS URL ending in <code>/FeatureServer/0</code> (replace 0 with the layer ID). Use a Query-only layer with pagination and GeoJSON support. Map the local image-path field and optionally native geometry. Enter an exact numeric object ID to look up one feature. For area search, leave the ID blank and enter a WGS84 rectangle or copy the map bounds. Area search verifies point and polygon footprints. For a layer that declares one UTC time field, reconnect to enable an inclusive UTC date filter. Layer time is recorded separately from any mapped image acquisition time. After preview, select and approve only the rows you want to index.</p><p>For a private STAC catalog backed by a local mount, map <code>properties.local_path</code> or an explicit <code>assets.&lt;key&gt;.href</code>. Use a path to a product folder or a complete multiband raster. Relative paths require an approved local root. Remote asset URLs stay unavailable for this import. Review and approve the selected local rows. To group separate single-band files as one product, enter a mapping such as <code>RED=assets.red.href, GREEN=assets.green.href</code> and supply the approved local root. Preview requires all chosen science assets. A bundle can contain up to 128 explicitly mapped single-band assets. Paste comma-separated mappings across multiple lines. For large bundles, use an exact item or a narrow collection preview. Enter an exact STAC item ID to retrieve a known item directly, or leave it blank for a collection preview. For OGC API Features or Records, enter an exact feature or record ID, or leave it blank and filter a collection by area. Area filtering checks returned point and polygon footprints. OGC Records also supports an inclusive UTC date window on the record time. This filter keeps the record time separate from any mapped image acquisition timestamp. For a date-filtered preview, leave the item ID blank and enter both UTC dates. The preview includes both dates and reads only one bounded page. To filter by location, enter a non-wrapping WGS84 rectangle or copy the bounds of a map polygon or rectangle. Preview sends those bounds to your selected private catalog. The rectangle can be combined with the UTC dates. Use <b>Find asset keys</b> to see keys for band mapping. This list contains item IDs and keys only. It follows the exact item ID or the area and UTC date filters entered above. Declared scale, offset and nodata are checked before indexing. Declared asset center wavelengths are converted from micrometres to nanometres. Conflicts with the raster header require review. Declared bandwidth is retained in catalog provenance. For a multiband raster in one asset, map its asset path. Declared channel names and wavelengths are kept in file order. A STAC scale and offset can calibrate a channel whose raster header uses the default scale 1 and offset 0. Conflicting non-default raster calibration or nodata blocks approval.</p>`},
    {id:'portable-algorithm-onboarding',group:'Analysis',title:'Qualify a portable land-cover model',summary:'Inspect and qualify a fixed, data-only model for manual and automatic inference.',keywords:'algorithm onboarding portable LULC runtime JSON coefficients bands validation qualification supplier offline',html:`<ol><li>Open a mission with a validation job and connected source imagery. In <b>Data &amp; results → Services &amp; workers → Algorithm onboarding</b>, inspect the package inventory with <b>Land cover</b> selected.</li><li>Verify the file checksums and choose <b>Prepare portable LULC for validation</b>. Confirm usage rights and that the imagery and labels were held out of supplier fitting and tuning. Select the job and set the minimum precision, recall, F1 and validation pixel count.</li><li>In <b>Training &amp; qualification</b>, add human validation references for every class, each assigned to its source image. Choose <b>Run independent validation</b>. Review the results and explicitly release the model if it meets the goals.</li><li>Use the qualified model in <b>Manual inference</b> or an <b>Automatic pipelines</b> algorithm block. Use <b>Revalidate</b> to return it to qualification and block operational use while retaining existing outputs.</li></ol><details><summary>Supported package format</summary><p>The inventory schema is <code>geo-intelligence-algorithm-inventory-v1</code>. Use task <code>lulc</code> and list one model JSON file with role <code>config</code> and a license file with role <code>license</code>. The JSON example below illustrates the schema. Its numbers are examples only.</p><pre>{"schema":"geo-intelligence-linear-lulc-v1","classes":["Water","Trees"],"sensors":["sentinel-2"],"processing_levels":["L2A"],"bands":["RED"],"ranges":[[0,1]],"coefficients":[[-10],[10]],"intercepts":[4.5,-4.5]}</pre><p>Each coefficient row and intercept correspond to one class in the declared order. Band names match the raster engine’s mapped names. Ranges use the engine’s scaled values. Use <code>UNSPECIFIED</code> only for intentionally untagged products. The softmax scores are uncalibrated. The CPU runtime scores fixed coefficients per valid pixel. Supplier scripts can be inspected as text but never run. Other model formats need their own reviewed adapters.</p></details>`},
    {id:'world-file',group:'Imagery',title:'Validate a world file',summary:'Verify a supplier affine transform with independent control points.',keywords:'world file TFW JGW WLD triangulation affine CRS',html:`<p>In <b>Image ↔ ground</b>, enter the absolute world-file path, open <b>Validate a geolocation sidecar</b>, and supply the image dimensions, EPSG code and at least four independent control points spread across the image. Use continuous column and row coordinates from the upper-left corner for the check points. The service accounts for the world file’s pixel-centre convention.</p><p>Review the residual and approve the candidate to enable conversion. The saved recipe is bound to the source file checksum.</p>`},
    {id:'rpc-sidecars',group:'Imagery',title:'Validate an RPC sidecar',summary:'Approve an RPB or GDAL/GeoEye RPC text file using independent 3D control points.',keywords:'RPB RPC00B _RPC.TXT GeoEye DIMAP Pléiades SPOT XML geolocation rational polynomial height ellipsoid pixel datum',html:`<h3>Convert pixel detections</h3><p>Open a geolocation configuration and choose <b>Convert pixel detections</b>. Supply a Shapefile with its .shx and .dbf files, or a .geojson FeatureCollection containing two-dimensional image column,row coordinates and no declared CRS. Confirm the file belongs to the selected image. Use the selected model and enter ellipsoidal height when required. The app converts vertices and saves a new geographic GeoJSON in the private workspace. Choose <b>Download GeoJSON</b> after conversion. Use <b>Browse saved vectors</b> to find and download older results.</p><h3>Validate embedded geolocation</h3><p>Inspect a GeoTIFF or JP2. If an affine, GCP or RPC model is found, open <b>Validate embedded geolocation</b>, enter at least four independent checkpoints, and approve the version after the residuals pass. RPC checkpoints also require ellipsoidal height in metres. GCP checkpoints must be distinct from the fitting points. This GCP conversion is planar and uses no height. For GCPs in a GDAL <code>.aux.xml</code> sidecar, use <b>Validate a geolocation sidecar</b> and enter the image dimensions. A supported sidecar contains one dataset-level GCP list with an offline EPSG coordinate system.</p><p>In <b>Services &amp; workers → Image ↔ ground</b>, enter the absolute path to an RPC00B <code>.rpb</code> file under an approved source folder. Open <b>Validate a geolocation sidecar</b> and give the original image width and height.</p><p>Provide four or more independent checkpoints distributed across the image. Use continuous column and row from the upper-left corner, WGS84 longitude and latitude, and the point’s ellipsoidal height in metres. Each JSON entry has the form <code>{"pixel":[120.5,85.5],"ground":[34.81,32.07],"height_metres":53.0}</code>. Enter the maximum allowed error in pixels. These example numbers illustrate the fields.</p><p>Choose <b>Validate sidecar</b>, review the measured residual for the supplied checkpoints, then approve the candidate. For conversion, enter an explicit ellipsoidal height. Revalidate if the sidecar changes. Results depend on the height and the quality of the supplier model and check points.</p><p>GDAL/GeoEye numbered text sidecars use <code>.rpc</code> or <code>_RPC.TXT</code> and the same control-point and approval workflow. Unknown coefficient layouts require a reviewed parser.</p><p>For a JSON or XML affine example, open <b>Register a JSON or XML affine format</b>, enter independent control points and choose <b>Suggest &amp; validate mapping</b>. The agent looks for one recognizable group of transform, EPSG code and image dimensions. Review the validated field mapping and approve when it matches the sample. If more than one mapping is possible, enter the paths explicitly. For XML, include the root element in each literal dotted path, such as <code>Vendor.Header.GeoTransform</code>. Use one text element per value and a declared EPSG code. Select the coefficient order when you enter a mapping manually. XML links and attributes are ignored.</p><p>For a full-scene Pléiades, Pléiades Neo, SPOT 6 or SPOT 7 DIMAP RPC product, supply the <code>RPC_*.XML</code> sidecar and dimensions of the entire scene. Validate with full-scene pixel coordinates. For a non-overlapping regular tile, expand <b>DIMAP tile coordinates</b> and provide the matching <code>DIM_*.XML</code> and tile filename. Enter the actual tile dimensions, including a smaller edge tile, and supply tile-local control points. Both metadata files are checked again at approval and conversion.</p>`},
    {id:'unet-workers',group:'Analysis',title:'Train one U-Net on enrolled workers',summary:'Use two to four private CPU or GPU workers with checkpoint recovery.',keywords:'U-Net distributed worker CUDA MPS GPU CPU restart checkpoint training validation',html:`<p>In Services &amp; workers → Worker pool, enroll approved workers, turn on routing, and choose two to four workers under <b>U-Net training</b>. Remote workers connect over internal HTTPS with a verified certificate. Each worker needs a 5 GiB RAM budget. Compatible CUDA or MPS devices with at least 4 GiB available in the enrollment budget are preferred, with CPU workers eligible.</p><p>In <b>Training &amp; qualification</b>, choose a U-Net configuration with reviewed labels and separate validation references. Select up to eight source images and a grid of 384 pixels or less. Each round combines the fitting gradients from every assigned worker before it saves the candidate checkpoint. Validation references and their pixel values are excluded from worker fitting inputs. Validation runs on the coordinator.</p><p>Pause and stop use safe checkpoints. During a restart, the coordinator checks the last completed barrier and reuses verified gradient contributions. Restart an assigned worker under its existing enrollment to resume that partition. Review Precision, Recall and F1 before releasing the candidate. Training alone does not release it.</p>`},
    {id:'detection-experiments',group:'Analysis',title:'Improve a rotated object detector',summary:'Compare a new few-shot candidate against a baseline on independent images.',keywords:'object detection rotated box aircraft vehicle few-shot support validation AP50 precision recall',html:'<ol><li>In Manual inference, open <b>Detection experiments</b>. Use <b>Open rotated box labeling</b> to save human-reviewed training boxes for each object class. Use 3–10 distinct examples per class.</li><li>On a different acquisition, label the same classes as <b>Validation</b>. Select that image and choose <b>Build &amp; compare candidate</b>. The app saves the candidate and compares nearest examples with a class-centroid baseline on the same rotated boxes.</li><li>In Progress &amp; results, choose <b>Evaluate against validation boxes</b>. For a complete image annotation, confirm you marked all target objects. The review reports per-class precision, recall, F1 and AP50 and shows whether precision reaches 80%. Partial annotation supports miss diagnosis.</li><li>Use the report to decide whether to improve box proposals, add examples for confused classes, or review duplicates. Use <b>Evaluate saved candidate</b> on another independently labeled acquisition to test the same weights without fitting again.</li></ol><p>The proposal detector limits which objects can be found. The reviewed example is not an operational release. Experiments use local source pixels and local model packs, and retain their GeoJSON and evidence.</p>'},
    {id:'dsen2',group:'Analysis',title:'Enhance native Sentinel-2 bands with DSen2',summary:'Export model-enhanced 20 m or 60 m science bands on the native 10 m guide grid.',keywords:'super-resolution DSen2 SAFE Sentinel L1C native calibration 10m 20m 60m enhanced GeoTIFF',html:`<p>Import a local Sentinel-2 L1C SAFE product with its original calibration XML and native grids. In <b>Manual inference</b>, choose the DSen2 20 m or 60 m model. The 60 m model also needs the 20 m bands. Run over the job area and download the continuous GeoTIFF from Progress &amp; results.</p><p>In <b>Automatic pipelines</b>, select Super-resolution with a local catalog and connect DSen2 to a GeoTIFF export block. The result preserves original band resolution in its report. The 10 m grid contains model estimates. The published test scenes were scored at reduced resolutions, so accuracy at the native 10 m output grid remains unmeasured.</p>`},
    {"id": "services-catalog", "group": "Data and catalog", "title": "Connect a private catalog", "summary": "Discover read-only image catalogs, map their fields and approve local imagery imports.", "keywords": "database Oracle SQL Server MySQL PostgreSQL MongoDB CSV GeoJSON STAC GIS local imports catalog connector service", "html": "<p>Open <b>Data &amp; results → Services &amp; workers → Catalog connections</b> and connect using the local workspace access key. Choose a supported engine, enter the internal endpoint and use an existing read-only account. MySQL 8.4 requires verified TLS, including on the same computer. SQL Server 2022, SQL Server 2025 and Oracle AI Database 26ai (23.26.2 or newer within 26ai) require an approved PEM CA file and the offline catalog connector component 1.2.0. SQL Server requires a dedicated SQL login with direct SELECT grants and database VIEW DEFINITION. Choose the entry matching the server version. SQL Server 2025 rechecks the recorded build before each preview. Oracle requires CREATE SESSION and direct READ grants. Oracle connects to the TCPS listener. PostgreSQL with PostGIS 3.5 can map native 2D EPSG:4326 geometry. Approved imports retain it as supplier provenance and derive the operational footprint from the raster.</p><ol><li>Read the available tables or collections. Map the path and any image identifier, date or other metadata fields.</li><li>Read a preview and tick the images to inspect. If supplier paths differ from the paths visible here, map the supplier prefix to an approved local folder.</li><li>Review the products and approve the import. Open <b>Local imagery</b> to use them. This does not enable folder monitoring.</li></ol><p>For an unlisted engine, use an existing read-only STAC endpoint for metadata discovery or inspect an existing UTF-8 CSV export with accessible image paths. Use <b>My database engine isn’t listed</b> to save product and version details locally. A pending record does not establish a live connection. Supplier systems are read-only; credentials and learned mappings stay inside the workspace.</p><p>For an existing GeoJSON FeatureCollection, enter the absolute <code>.geojson</code> path in the catalog inspector. Map the property that holds the local image path. Optional fields <code>@id</code> and <code>@geometry</code> read the feature identifier and two-dimensional CRS84 footprint. Preview the rows, choose the images, and approve the import. If the export changes before approval, inspect it again.</p>"},
    {"id": "sam3-offline", "group": "Analysis", "title": "SAM 3 interactive segmentation", "summary": "Local point/box proposals with no account or web API.", "keywords": "SAM3 SAM 3 offline component installer segmentation points box memory", "html": "<p>SAM 3 Tracker replaces the active SAM 2.1 adapter. It uses a publicly downloadable ONNX Community conversion with a quantized vision encoder and a full-precision mask decoder. No account, online API or runtime model download is used.</p><p>On Windows, install the separate <b>S3/3.exe</b> component with its adjacent payload folder, then the small application update. This preserves the stable base prerequisites. Point and box prompts work on a mapped three-band display composite. Text-concept prompting is not included in this adapter.</p><p>The worker uses a supported dedicated CUDA GPU when available in the runtime, otherwise CPU. CPU inference needs substantial working memory\u2014about 8 GiB free is recommended. A free-memory check reports insufficient RAM instead of substituting another model.</p><p>Proposals remain unclassified, and model quality scores are not measured accuracy. Assign a class and explicitly save your decision to create a reference. SAM fine-tuning remains unsupported. Historical SAM 2 tasks and outputs retain their original model identity and cannot be resumed with SAM 3 weights.</p>"},
    {"id": "landsat-mtl", "group": "Analysis", "title": "Landsat calibration metadata", "summary": "Import the supplier MTL with Landsat 8/9 Collection-2 Level-2 bands.", "keywords": "Landsat MTL scale offset calibration thermal temperature reflectance", "html": "<p>Keep the original <code>*_MTL.txt</code>, XML or JSON beside the Landsat band folders. The importer reads the declared Level-2 coefficients, rather than the different Level-1 coefficients stored elsewhere in the same MTL.</p><p>Surface reflectance bands retain their individual scale/offset. ST_B10 is retained as temperature in kelvin. Thermal auxiliary, quality, mask and preview files do not become separate catalog images.</p><p>For a previously saved uncalibrated Landsat source, open <b>Source data</b> and use <b>Attach Landsat MTL</b>. The metadata must match every saved band. This creates a new input recipe; earlier runs and original band pixels remain unchanged. Start a new analysis to use the repaired recipe.</p><p>Dynamic World requires Sentinel L1C. Prithvi Crop needs a genuine three-date HLS stack. OpenEarthMap needs 0.25\u20130.5 m RGB; it cannot use ordinary Sentinel/Landsat pixels.</p>"},
    {"id": "image-comparison", "group": "Analysis", "title": "Compare an image with its result", "summary": "Swipe, zoom, opacity, pixel inspection and rectangular corrections without a geographic map.", "keywords": "swipe divider image original result zoom pan mission delete thumbnail mask name", "html": "<p>In a mission, open <b>Runs &amp; results</b>, then <b>Open image &amp; classification</b> or <b>Open image &amp; agreement</b>. The large image desk places the saved source RGB beneath the result; it does not add either layer to the map.</p><ol><li>Drag the white divider or use the Swipe slider. Only the dividing line moves.</li><li>Scroll or use +/\u2212 to zoom. Drag the image to pan. Both layers move together. <b>Fit image</b> resets the view; <b>Expand</b> fills the screen.</li><li>Change <b>Result opacity</b> to see through the overlay. These are display settings; scientific raster values do not change.</li><li>Click the image to inspect saved model votes. For a correction choose <b>Use clicked pixel</b>, or <b>Draw image region</b> and drag a rectangle. Choose a class and reference role, then save.</li></ol><p>RGB is a bounded display preview with a percentile stretch; pixel inspection and saved corrections use the georeferenced analysis grid. The original science bands and model outputs are preserved.</p><p>To delete a mission without opening it, use <b>Delete mission</b> on its mission-list card. Confirmation explains that active runs are stopped and saved imagery, results and corrections are retained.</p><p>The catalog lists logical image products, not standalone thumbnails, quality files or masks. Mission and result views keep the supplier/product name shown in the catalog; internal IDs are used only for database relationships.</p>"},
    {"id": "algorithm-satellites", "group": "Analysis", "title": "Which satellites suit each algorithm?", "summary": "Satellite families, required bands, product levels, training needs and limitations for all registered algorithms.", "keywords": "algorithms satellite Sentinel Landsat HLS hyperspectral Hyperion EnMAP PRISMA WorldView Pleiades suitability compatibility", "html": "<p>Suitability describes the current input adapter. It is not a measured accuracy claim for every satellite. Sensor-flexible models require suitable training references; released pretrained heads have narrower product, band and resolution requirements. A supported file format alone does not establish sensor compatibility.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Satellites / sensors</th><th>Required input</th><th>Limits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>Random Forest</th><td>Sensor-flexible · Sentinel-2, Landsat and mapped hyperspectral GeoTIFFs such as Hyperion, EnMAP or PRISMA</td><td>Consistent band identities, units and masks; labeled examples for the sensor and class taxonomy.</td><td>A trainable architecture, not a ready pretrained model for every satellite. Sensors without an adapter require prepared local GeoTIFFs; accuracy must be validated.</td></tr><tr><th>Support Vector Machine</th><td>Sensor-flexible · Sentinel-2, Landsat and mapped hyperspectral GeoTIFFs such as Hyperion, EnMAP or PRISMA</td><td>Consistent band identities, units and masks; labeled examples for the sensor and class taxonomy.</td><td>A trainable architecture, not a ready pretrained model for every satellite. Sensors without an adapter require prepared local GeoTIFFs; accuracy must be validated.</td></tr><tr><th>XGBoost</th><td>Sensor-flexible · Sentinel-2, Landsat and mapped hyperspectral GeoTIFFs such as Hyperion, EnMAP or PRISMA</td><td>Consistent band identities, units and masks; labeled examples for the sensor and class taxonomy.</td><td>A trainable architecture, not a ready pretrained model for every satellite. Sensors without an adapter require prepared local GeoTIFFs; accuracy must be validated.</td></tr><tr><th>U-Net</th><td>Sensor-flexible · Sentinel-2, Landsat and mapped hyperspectral GeoTIFFs such as Hyperion, EnMAP or PRISMA</td><td>Mapped bands and spatial training masks; train or adapt for the sensor, resolution and classes.</td><td>A trainable architecture, not a ready pretrained model for every satellite. Sensors without an adapter require prepared local GeoTIFFs; accuracy must be validated.</td></tr><tr><th>Prithvi-EO-2.0-300M</th><td>HLS-like Sentinel-2 and Landsat 8/9 reflectance</td><td>BLUE, GREEN, RED, narrow NIR, SWIR1 and SWIR2; mapped physical reflectance and labeled classes. Sentinel-2 NIR uses B8A.</td><td>HLS 30 m is the pretrained input domain. This adapter freezes the encoder and trains a downstream head; it does not use a full hyperspectral cube.</td></tr><tr><th>LULC Ensemble</th><td>Sentinel-2 / Landsat with Prithvi-compatible reflectance bands</td><td>The full five-member ensemble needs the six Prithvi bands, labeled classes and compatible member taxonomies/grids.</td><td>It inherits its members’ restrictions. Use individual sensor-flexible models for RGB-only or unmapped hyperspectral inputs.</td></tr><tr><th>Dynamic World · pretrained LULC</th><td>Sentinel-2 MSI · Level-1C</td><td>TOA reflectance; B02, B03, B04, B05, B06, B07, B08, B11 and B12 on a 10 m analysis grid.</td><td>Not Landsat or L2A. Locally exercised on one Sentinel-2A product; this is not an accuracy guarantee for every scene.</td></tr><tr><th>Prithvi crop/LULC · pretrained head</th><td>Landsat and Sentinel-2 through HLS L30 / S30</td><td>Three real HLS acquisition dates, six surface-reflectance bands per date, 18 channels total at 30 m.</td><td>Raw Landsat or Sentinel products are not automatically HLS. One date repeated three times is not valid input.</td></tr><tr><th>OpenEarthMap · pretrained U-Net</th><td>Very high resolution RGB · e.g. suitable WorldView / Pleiades products; aerial and UAV orthophotos</td><td>Georeferenced RGB with actual 0.25–0.5 m source detail and compatible radiometry.</td><td>Not Sentinel-2 or Landsat. Upsampling coarse imagery does not create the required detail. Validate transfer to your imagery.</td></tr><tr><th>Regularized RX</th><td>Multispectral / hyperspectral · Sentinel-2, Landsat, Hyperion, EnMAP or PRISMA with mapped local rasters</td><td>All connected science bands on a common grid; meaningful background pixels and consistent calibration.</td><td>Fits background statistics for your dataset. Anomaly scores do not identify a semantic class or establish detection accuracy.</td></tr><tr><th>Local RX</th><td>Multispectral / hyperspectral · Sentinel-2, Landsat, Hyperion, EnMAP or PRISMA with mapped local rasters</td><td>All connected science bands on a common grid; meaningful background pixels and consistent calibration.</td><td>Fits background statistics for your dataset. Anomaly scores do not identify a semantic class or establish detection accuracy.</td></tr><tr><th>Isolation Forest</th><td>Multispectral / hyperspectral · Sentinel-2, Landsat, Hyperion, EnMAP or PRISMA with mapped local rasters</td><td>All connected science bands on a common grid; meaningful background pixels and consistent calibration.</td><td>Fits background statistics for your dataset. Anomaly scores do not identify a semantic class or establish detection accuracy.</td></tr><tr><th>Spectral autoencoder</th><td>Multispectral / hyperspectral · Sentinel-2, Landsat, Hyperion, EnMAP or PRISMA with mapped local rasters</td><td>All connected science bands on a common grid; meaningful background pixels and consistent calibration.</td><td>Spectral reconstruction model fitted to your background; not a pretrained satellite classifier.</td></tr><tr><th>SAM 3 Tracker</th><td>Any sensor with a mapped three-band display composite · Sentinel-2, Landsat, VHR or hyperspectral-derived RGB</td><td>Three explicitly selected display bands plus foreground points or a box prompt.</td><td>Uses three bands, not the full spectrum. Detail is limited by ground resolution. Proposes boundaries, not LULC classes; human class approval is required.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Primary model documentation: <a href=\"https://github.com/google/dynamicworld\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dynamic World</a>, <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/ibm-nasa-geospatial/Prithvi-EO-2.0-300M\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Prithvi EO 2.0</a>, <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/ibm-nasa-geospatial/Prithvi-EO-1.0-100M-multi-temporal-crop-classification\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Prithvi crop</a>, <a href=\"https://open-earth-map.org/overview.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenEarthMap</a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/facebookresearch/sam3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">SAM 3</a>. These links require internet; the guidance above is bundled locally.</p>"},
    {
      id:'supplier-calibration',group:'Imagery',title:'Import bands and supplier calibration',
      summary:'Keep each acquisition and its calibration metadata together.',
      keywords:'Sentinel SAFE XML MTD_MSIL1C reflectance DN offset quantification Landsat bundle separate bands MTL',
      html:`<p>A multiband image can be one file or several band files. Use <b>Import folder</b> to keep the supplier's directory structure and filenames. Bands from one acquisition are grouped as one product; different acquisitions remain separate. Inspect the detected bands before adding them to a job.</p><p><b>Sentinel SAFE:</b> include <code>MTD_MSIL1C.xml</code> or <code>MTD_MSIL2A.xml</code>. The application reads the declared quantification value and per-band offsets and saves the metadata with the job's band snapshots. Source pixels remain unchanged. DN zero stays nodata; negative calibrated reflectance is retained.</p><p>If an older import omitted its XML, open <b>Source data</b> and choose <b>Attach SAFE metadata</b> on the matching image. The product identity is checked. This creates a new input version and preserves earlier runs. Run the analysis again to produce new outputs.</p><p><b>Landsat grouping:</b> retain matching names such as <code>LC08_…_SR_B1.TIF</code> through the other bands of that same acquisition, plus its MTL file. Band folders may be siblings under one selected parent. A grouped product is not, by itself, proof of correct radiometric calibration. MTL files are retained; the SAFE XML attachment tool is specifically for Sentinel products.</p>`
    },
    {
      id:'mission-workflow',group:'Start here',title:'Mission workflow and image review',
      summary:'Choose images, configure source data, run analysis, and review results over the original image.',
      keywords:'mission pause resume stop remove delete thumbnail footprint native 10m grid Dynamic World results original image opacity swipe',
      html:`<ol><li><b>Manual inference:</b> choose a job, images and a compatible released model. Source thumbnails appear when available.</li><li><b>Source data:</b> connect the actual bands using saved local files, streaming or download. The mission guide shows how many images are ready.</li><li><b>Runs &amp; results:</b> follow processing progress and open a completed result.</li><li><b>Review on image:</b> see the classification above a stretched RGB display of its saved source image. Use the image viewer’s opacity and swipe controls. Inspect pixels and save explicit corrections.</li></ol><p><b>Pause</b> waits for a safe checkpoint. <b>Resume</b> continues a paused worker. <b>Stop</b> preserves completed artifacts and cancels unfinished work at its next checkpoint. Resuming a stopped mission enables new runs; it does not restart cancelled runs. <b>Remove mission</b> stops active work and hides the mission while preserving its data locally.</p><p>Dynamic World inference on Sentinel-2 L1C automatically processes the full chosen AOI in bounded windows at 10 m. The grid selector controls block size for this model, not final resolution. Coarser science bands are resampled to that grid; original bands are preserved. A 10 m output does not add native detail to a coarser band. Thumbnails and opacity are display features and never alter analytical rasters.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'desktop', group: 'Start here', title: 'Offline desktop installation',
      summary: 'Install and use the self-contained Windows or Mac desktop edition.',
      keywords: 'desktop offline windows mac installer setup wizard local imagery GPU CPU USB installation',
      html: `<h3>Install from prepared media</h3><p>The desktop application includes its own window, browser engine, Python, local model runtime, pretrained weights, map scripts and fonts. No separate Docker, Python, browser or Ollama installation is required.</p><ol><li><b>Windows x64:</b> copy the complete <code>SS-0225</code> folder and keep each payload beside its wizard. For a new Current User installation run <code>1.exe</code>, then <code>2.exe</code>, then the detector wizards <code>D1/4.exe</code> and <code>D2/5.exe</code> if you want Detection experiments. Use the same installation directory throughout. On an existing compatible installation, stop the app and run <code>2.exe</code> for a code-only update. Install any missing detector packs separately. The wizards use your Windows user profile and app-local Microsoft DLLs. The older machine-wide updater is separate.</li><li><b>Mac Apple Silicon, macOS 14 or later:</b> use a verified matching Mac installer when one is available. The existing installation folder and workspace name remain SpectraScope.</li><li>Windows: choose your workspace and LLM preference in the installation/update wizard. It verifies files and checks native libraries before Finish. Later launches start services only. The older Mac installer may request these settings on first launch. Interrupted application setup offers Resume setup; rerun an interrupted Windows update to recover and resume verification. Only an algorithm you actually run prepares its writable model cache.</li></ol><h3>Profiles and storage</h3><p>Automatic selection uses Qwen3.5 2B on a 16 GB machine and 4B on machines with at least 24 GB. The 9B option is intended for 48 GB or more. All three model packs are supplied; switching among them requires no download. Compatible dedicated GPUs are used when supported; otherwise analysis uses CPU. On Apple Silicon, supported native inference can use MPS. Incompatible devices use CPU. A Windows NVIDIA GPU needs a compatible driver already installed.</p><h3>Bring your imagery</h3><p>Choose <b>Import local imagery</b> to copy a georeferenced GeoTIFF or JPEG2000 into your workspace and select it for a mission. Local imports can be selected for new jobs. Ready monitored products populate the Local imagery table automatically, including while the monitoring dialog is closed. In <b>Data &amp; results → Source data</b>, verify band names, product type, reflectance units and temporal dates before running a compatible model. The import date is recorded as an import time, not a claimed satellite acquisition date. Recognized products and explicit band manifests can supply acquisition dates; unknown acquisition times remain labeled as import times.</p><h3>Automatic online maps and catalogs</h3><p>After installation completes, Geo Intelligence checks the built-in street-map, Esri satellite/aerial and satellite-catalog services. When connected, select Sentinel-2 or Landsat normally; the existing map providers are available without entering a URL. The top bar shows connection status and application version. If services become unreachable, the map uses the bundled world map. Your chosen catalog tab, results and selections stay in place. Online search pauses; Local imagery remains available in its own tab. Reachable services reconnect automatically without switching your tab. Checks run about every 30 seconds. A firewall, proxy, sign-in page or provider outage can leave individual services unavailable even when the computer has a network connection.</p><p>Installation/native validation and model packs stay local. Online mode uses the installed model packs and an approved internal model service. For explicit offline-only use, stop local services and launch with <code>--offline</code>; restart without that flag to restore automatic connectivity.</p><h3>Offline maps and catalogs</h3><p>Choose <b>World · Light</b> or <b>World · Dark</b> in the basemap menu. <b>Map theme</b> changes control appearance (Light, Dark or Follow system). These are separate display choices. Offline catalog and raster access options explain their availability; they do not contact online services.</p><p>The supplied Natural Earth basemap contains world boundaries, not global satellite imagery. Use <b>Add map / orthophoto → Use your own image</b> for a georeferenced local basemap. Live public catalogs, remote streaming, remote map-service URLs and non-loopback model endpoints are disabled in the strict offline edition. Import imagery downloaded on an authorized connected system beforehand.</p><h3>Your workspace survives upgrades</h3><p>Missions, corrections, trained candidates, source images, result layers and exports are stored in your selected workspace, separately from installed software. Back up that complete folder with Geo Intelligence closed. Uninstalling the desktop shell does not delete it. The installation media contains software and public model weights; it does not include your existing private missions or keys.</p><h3>Startup problems</h3><p>Read the setup error and use <b>Help → Show logs</b> in the desktop menu. Missing or damaged components require a complete verified copy of the offline media; the desktop will not repair them by downloading. Unsigned build candidates require your organization to approve their installation. Windows installation and GPU behavior require acceptance testing on your Windows PC before deployment.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'start', group: 'Start here', title: 'Your first analysis',
      summary: 'A practical route from satellite discovery to a reviewed result.',
      keywords: 'quick start beginner workflow tutorial help overview navigation',
      html: `<div class="guide-callout"><strong>Three things to keep separate</strong><p>A catalog record locates an image. Raster access connects its actual pixels. An inference report contains the analysis. Finding a scene does not automatically download or classify it.</p></div>
        <h3>The workspace at a glance</h3><div class="guide-flow"><span>Find imagery</span><span>Mission & job</span><span>Connect rasters</span><span>Run models</span><span>Review & learn</span></div>
        <ol><li><strong>Find imagery.</strong> Select Online catalogs or Local imagery, define an AOI and acquisition dates, then search or browse the local library.</li><li><strong>Make a job.</strong> Select scenes and choose <b>Add selected scenes to a mission</b>. Create a mission or add a job to an existing one.</li><li><strong>Connect pixels.</strong> In <b>Data &amp; results → Source data</b>, select the job and connect its science rasters. Images added from Local imagery can already have saved band snapshots.</li><li><strong>Run a released model.</strong> In <b>Manual inference</b> select the job, images and compatible model. Choose <b>Run selected</b>. For your own classes, begin in <b>Training &amp; qualification</b>.</li><li><strong>Review.</strong> Open <b>Runs &amp; results</b> or review the completed run from Manual inference. Use <b>Activity &amp; Logs</b> for progress and errors.</li></ol>
        <h3>A straightforward first example</h3><p>For label-free initial LULC inference, search <b>Sentinel-2 · Level-1C · pretrained LULC</b> and use <b>Dynamic World</b> alone. It needs the nine documented bands and native 10 m source support. Start with a small AOI and 192 px or 384 px analysis grid. Other sensors or processing levels require a different compatible model.</p>
        <p>For unusual spectra rather than semantic classes, open <b>Spectral anomalies</b> after configuring raster access. You can begin with scene background and no labeled anomalies.</p>
        <h3>Get help as you work</h3><p>Choose <b>Help</b> in the top bar or press <kbd>F1</kbd> while focus is in the main application, outside the embedded map. The small <b>?</b> buttons beside section headings open the relevant chapter. Search by a task, control name or error. Choose <b>Full manual</b> for printing, or <b>Save offline copy</b> for a complete HTML document.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'search', group: 'Discover', title: 'Catalog search & results',
      summary: 'Choose a source, acquisition dates and cloud limit; select the scenes to investigate.',
      keywords: 'Sentinel Landsat Hyperion STAC date cloud footprint pagination empty missing search results selection',
      html: `<h3>Catalog choices</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Catalog</th><th>What to use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sentinel-2 Level-2A</td><td>Surface-reflectance multispectral imagery for compatible trained classifiers.</td></tr><tr><td>Sentinel-2 Level-1C</td><td>Top-of-atmosphere input for the native Dynamic World adapter.</td></tr><tr><td>Landsat Collection 2 Level-2</td><td>USGS surface-reflectance scene discovery; check source asset access and mapped bands.</td></tr><tr><td>EO-1 Hyperion Level 1T</td><td>Historical hyperspectral discovery. Archived bundles may need local extraction into a georeferenced stack.</td></tr></tbody></table>
        <ol><li>Select <b>Data source</b> and define an <a href="#aoi" data-help-topic="aoi">AOI</a>.</li><li>Set <b>From</b> and <b>To</b> to the acquisition period. The initial range is the last 30 days; it is not a guarantee of coverage.</li><li>Set <b>Max cloud cover</b>. A lower limit returns fewer scenes. Catalog cloud cover is usually scene-level metadata and does not prove your AOI is cloud-free.</li><li>Choose <b>Search catalog</b>. Read the status message, footprints and table.</li><li>Use <b>Load more catalog results</b> when available. The first page is not the entire archive. Footprint gaps are not proof that no imagery exists.</li></ol>
        <h3>Online and Local tabs</h3><p>The results panel has separate <b>Online catalogs</b> and <b>Local imagery</b> tabs. Each retains its results and selected images when you switch tabs or reconnect. Local monitoring updates its tab while you work online. The selection summary shows online and local counts together; <b>Add selected scenes to a mission</b> creates one job from both. Changing the online data-source provider starts a fresh result list for that provider; it does not clear the Local tab.</p><h3>Reading the table</h3><p>Rows identify scenes, sensor/catalog, acquisition date and cloud metadata. Click a row to locate its footprint; use its checkbox to select it for a mission. <b>Select all</b> applies to the results currently loaded, not undiscovered archive pages. <b>Clear all</b> clears selections in both tabs. <b>Select all</b> selects only the visible tab. Thumbnails are previews, not the analysis raster.</p>
        <p>On wide displays the map and compact results list share the screen. On smaller displays use the <b>Map</b> and <b>Results</b> tabs. Selected scene metadata is carried into a job; it does not prove that remote science assets are publicly readable.</p>
        <h3>If results are empty</h3><p>Check that the AOI is complete, dates are in order and within the sensor's operating history, and cloud filtering is not too strict. Widen the dates, increase the cloud limit, load further pages or try another catalog. Read provider/network errors before changing the query. See <a href="#troubleshooting" data-help-topic="troubleshooting">troubleshooting</a>.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'aoi', group: 'Discover', title: 'Draw & use an AOI',
      summary: 'Rectangles, complex polygons, coordinate points and the area used by a job.',
      keywords: 'area interest polygon rectangle box point latitude longitude draw overlap geometry WGS84',
      html: `<h3>Rectangle or polygon</h3><ol><li>Use the drawing controls inside the map. Choose rectangle for a box or polygon for a custom outline.</li><li>Place the vertices and finish the geometry using the map tool's completion gesture. While tracing a complex boundary, keep adding vertices; the final outline should be an unambiguous, well-defined polygon.</li><li>Choose <b>Use drawn area</b> to read the latest completed drawing. Confirm that the AOI status describes the intended shape before searching.</li></ol>
        <h3>Point coordinates</h3><p>Enter <b>Longitude</b> first (−180 to 180), then <b>Latitude</b> (−90 to 90), in WGS84 decimal degrees. For example, longitude <code>35.21</code>, latitude <code>31.77</code>. Choose <b>Place point AOI</b>. Do not enter projected easting/northing values in these fields.</p>
        <div class="guide-callout"><strong>A point is not a processing polygon</strong><p>It can locate catalog scenes. Draw a polygon or rectangle when you want an explicit processing boundary; the raster reader does not crop a point to an invented user-specified radius.</p></div>
        <h3>Drawings, footprints and review regions</h3><p>The AOI is your chosen search or reference geometry. Catalog footprints show the acquisition extent of scenes. A review region is the pixel or drawing you explicitly approve as a reference. They serve different purposes even when displayed together.</p>
        <p>A new job stores the current AOI with the selected imagery. Redrawing a search area later does not silently redefine an existing job. Before starting analysis, check the job's area and imagery. A shape outside a raster's coverage cannot provide valid analysis pixels.</p>
        <p>If a drawing was not picked up, finish it, leave drawing mode if necessary, choose <b>Use drawn area</b> again and confirm the status. Avoid leaving a self-intersecting or unfinished final boundary.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'missions', group: 'Organize', title: 'Missions, jobs & images',
      summary: 'Create or edit a mission and organize selected scenes into separate analysis jobs.',
      keywords: 'mission job hierarchy edit rename new existing add images select saved plan',
      html: `<h3>The hierarchy</h3><div class="guide-flow"><span>Mission</span><span>One or more jobs</span><span>Images in each job</span><span>Runs & reports</span></div><p>A mission groups an investigation. A job groups imagery, its AOI, analysis choices and references. A run is one execution of a job; repeat runs produce separate records.</p>
        <h3>Create a mission with imagery</h3><ol><li>Check scenes in the catalog table and choose <b>Add selected scenes to a mission</b>.</li><li>Choose <b>Create a new mission</b>, enter a mission name and job name, then choose <b>Create mission & job</b>.</li><li>The mission detail opens with the job/image hierarchy.</li></ol>
        <h3>Add a job to an existing mission</h3><p>Select another group of catalog scenes. In the setup dialog choose <b>Add to an existing mission</b>, select that mission and name the new job. This creates another job under the mission. The <b>Add selected scenes</b> action uses the current catalog selection; return to the search workspace first if no scenes are selected.</p>
        <h3>Open and edit</h3><p>Choose <b>Mission workspace</b>, open a mission card, and use <b>Edit mission</b> to rename it. <b>All missions</b> returns to the mission list. <b>New mission</b> can create an empty planning container, but it will need a job and connected imagery before analysis.</p>
        <h3>Choose analysis targets</h3><p>Open <b>Manual inference</b>, choose a job, and select the source images. <b>Connect source pixels</b> opens Source data. Choose a model that supports the source, then choose <b>Run selected</b>.</p>
        <p><b>Automatic pipelines</b> schedules work from catalog matches, <b>Training &amp; qualification</b> releases candidates, and <b>Manual inference</b> applies released models. Use <b>Data &amp; results</b> for source images, review and saved layers.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'local-library', group: 'Discover', title: 'Local folders and multiband products',
      summary: 'Group band files into images and control folder monitoring with saved checkboxes.',
      keywords: 'local folder monitor watch checkbox catalog import hyperspectral multispectral bands separate files SAFE Landsat grouping',
      html: `<h3>Import one logical image with its bands</h3><p>Choose <b>Import local imagery</b> to select all source files, or <b>Import multiband folder</b> to select a complete directory tree. A multiband raster stays one image. Recognized scene-and-band filenames and sibling band folders are grouped into one image product. Separate acquisitions are kept separate. A browser cannot access sibling files you did not select; select the entire product folder when its bands are separate.</p><h3>The monitoring checkbox is the switch</h3><p>Use the prominent <b>Folder monitoring</b> panel on the main workspace, then choose <b>Manage folders &amp; image library</b>. Add an absolute local folder path on the computer running the app. <b>Monitor this folder</b> is saved exactly as checked or unchecked: checked starts continuous polling while local services run and resumes after restart; unchecked pauses monitoring. There is no automatic profile or hardware rule that overrides your checkbox. In the browser edition, local services remain in the notification area when the browser tab closes. When services are stopped, monitoring stops and catches up after restart if the checkbox is checked.</p><p>Checks normally run every 30 seconds; the confirmation scan for a changed folder runs after about two seconds. <b>Scan now</b> checks an enabled folder immediately. The index waits for file writes to settle and for the folder contents to remain stable across two scans. Large transfers can take longer. Last scan, file and product counts, scan status, errors and required reviews are visible beside each folder. A bad file no longer blocks unrelated valid acquisitions. An unreadable band holds its whole acquisition for review rather than silently dropping that band. Use <b>Browse all ready images</b> to show cataloged products without the AOI or date filters; an older acquisition can otherwise be excluded by your search dates. An unavailable folder retains its last catalog and reports an error; removing files from an available folder updates the active catalog after a stable scan. Removing a monitoring entry does not delete files or saved job inputs.</p><h3>Inspect the grouping before analysis</h3><p>Each library row shows its source-file count, channel count and expected-band completeness. Choose <b>Inspect bands</b> to see every selected file, native grid and channel names. Bare filenames, repeated bands, incomplete known products and conflicting footprints require explicit review. Select files, name every channel and confirm that they form one acquisition. Invalid or non-overlapping groups cannot be approved. If an expected band count is not declared, completeness is not asserted.</p><p>For Sentinel-2, alternative resolutions of a band are retained as alternatives and the finest matching grid is selected. Known quality, display and atmospheric retrieval files are listed as non-spectral exclusions. Science channels are never silently reduced. Native scales, offsets and nodata masks are preserved; verify units and product type before pretrained inference. Automatic grouping is not evidence that radiometric calibration or sensor compatibility has been verified. Declared scene/acquisition metadata can identify an image across otherwise unrelated filenames. Declared wavelength units are retained or explicitly converted to nm; missing units are not guessed.</p><h3>Custom hyperspectral layouts</h3><p>For other filenames, add <code>spectrascope-image.json</code> beside the band folders. Declare a product name, acquisition datetime, sensor and a <code>bands</code> list. Each entry has a relative <code>path</code> and <code>name</code>; optionally supply <code>wavelength_nm</code>, <code>scale</code> and <code>offset</code>. A multiband file is declared once with <code>band_names</code> in its actual channel order. A temporal Prithvi HLS product declares <code>processing_level: HLS</code> and three distinct actual <code>temporal_dates</code>, with the six HLS bands in date order. Repeated spectral names are allowed only for that declared temporal schema. <code>expected_bands</code> declares completeness. See the example in the library dialog. Paths cannot escape the imported folder or point to URLs.</p><h3>Saved inputs and storage</h3><p>Monitoring indexes files in place. Adding a product to a mission/job preserves immutable application-managed copies of every selected source band, with checksums, original paths, names and metadata. Changed files create catalog revisions; existing jobs retain their original snapshots. Copies use disk space only for selected content, with equal-content reuse. The shared reader aligns different native grids spatially and records resampling; the original bands remain intact. Changed or damaged source snapshots stop inference with an error.</p><h3>Formats and deployments</h3><p>Supported local science formats are georeferenced GeoTIFF and JPEG2000, not arbitrary ENVI/HDF/NetCDF products. Convert unsupported products with their georeferencing and band metadata preserved before importing. Manual uploads allow 5 GB per raster; local monitoring supports larger files. Processing grids have a memory budget and reject oversized full-band cubes rather than dropping spectral channels. In Docker, set <code>SPECTRASCOPE_IMAGERY_DIR</code> to a host parent directory before starting the stack; its read-only container path is <code>/app/imports</code>. Enter that container path, not an unmounted host path, in the library. Offline native editions accept local drives, not remote URLs, network shares or VRT inputs.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'rasters', group: 'Prepare imagery', title: 'Streaming, downloads & local files',
      summary: 'Connect real science pixels to the catalog scenes in a job.',
      keywords: 'raster access stream download local path upload GeoTIFF JP2 server Docker credentials HTTP ready',
      html: `<ol><li>Open a mission's <b>Data &amp; results → Source data</b> tab and choose the target job.</li><li>Select its source scenes. Choose an access mode, check band names and product type, and supply real temporal dates if required.</li><li>Choose <b>Configure selected scenes</b>. The backend probes the raster before saving a ready record. Read any per-scene error.</li></ol>
        <table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Behavior</th><th>Choose when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stream remote imagery</td><td>Reads source raster windows through GDAL; does not first save the full scene.</td><td>The provider permits windowed remote access and your network is reliable.</td></tr><tr><td>Download complete raster</td><td>Saves discovered science-raster assets in the local raster cache.</td><td>You need a reusable local copy and have enough disk space. Large downloads take time.</td></tr><tr><td>Use locally saved raster path</td><td>Reads a GeoTIFF or JPEG 2000 beneath the configured local raster root.</td><td>You have extracted, calibrated or privately supplied imagery.</td></tr></tbody></table>
        <h3>Local paths are server paths</h3><p>With the supplied Docker setup, put files under <code>data/spectrascope/local-rasters/</code> on the host. Enter a path relative to that folder, such as <code>survey/scene-01.tif</code>, for each selected image. A path elsewhere on your laptop is not automatically visible inside the container. The operator can configure <code>LOCAL_RASTER_ROOT</code>; paths must remain inside that root.</p>
        <h3>Preview upload versus analysis access</h3><p><b>Upload GeoTIFF</b> in the search sidebar adds an RGB or single-band preview to MC². It does not create an analysis job or attach the file to a job's raster-access record. For analysis of your own imagery, use a job image entry and connect the file with <b>Use locally saved raster path</b>. The current UI has no independent local-file-to-mission import wizard.</p>
        <p>For a preview, choose display mode and one-based band numbers before selecting the file. Use three valid indices for RGB or one for single band. Source-band order is not automatically RGB.</p>
        <h3>Protected or archived products</h3><p>Catalog metadata may be public while raster assets require credentials. Provider authentication is not bypassed. Use the provider’s approved download tools and import the resulting local files. Never paste secrets into scene names or notes. For unsupported archives, including some Hyperion bundles, extract the bands locally and provide a georeferenced multiband GeoTIFF.</p>
        <p>Ready states include <code>stream-ready</code>, <code>downloaded</code> and <code>local-ready</code>. A ready record means the probe succeeded at that time, not that the remote service will always be available. See <a href="#bands" data-help-topic="bands">band and product requirements</a>.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'bands', group: 'Prepare imagery', title: 'Bands, products & analysis grid',
      summary: 'Preserve spectral meaning and choose compatible inputs and spatial resolution.',
      keywords: 'multispectral hyperspectral band names NIR SWIR wavelength reflectance calibration TOA L1C L2A HLS RGB resolution grid CRS nodata',
      html: `<h3>Map band names to actual channels</h3><p>Enter comma-separated names in <b>Band names · optional</b> when the stack lacks trustworthy band descriptions. The number of names must match the raster's channels. For a six-band reflectance stack an example is <code>BLUE,GREEN,RED,NIR,SWIR1,SWIR2</code>. Never use this list for a differently ordered raster without checking its metadata.</p>
        <p>Sentinel-2 maps B2/B3/B4 to BLUE/GREEN/RED, B8A to narrow NIR, and B11/B12 to SWIR1/SWIR2. B8 and B8A are distinct inputs. Dynamic World additionally uses B5, B6, B7 and B8. Landsat band numbering differs; the adapter uses its catalog metadata to interpret common names.</p>
        <h3>Product type matters</h3><p><b>L1C</b> is top-of-atmosphere reflectance; <b>L2A</b> is surface reflectance. They are not interchangeable for a pretrained model. <b>HLS</b> temporal models need three genuine acquisition dates and the required 18-channel layout. <b>RGB</b> for OpenEarthMap means very-high-resolution imagery, not a stretched Sentinel-2 thumbnail. Setting a product label does not convert or calibrate the raster.</p>
        <h3>Spatial grid and no-data</h3><p>The raster reader windows the AOI, reprojects/resamples channels onto a shared grid, applies supported scale/offset metadata and retains validity masks. The analysis-grid value is the maximum longest-side pixel count, not a ground resolution in meters. Choosing 1024 px does not invent native detail or make an entire satellite scene run at full source resolution.</p>
        <p>Start at 192 or 384 px; increase to 768 or 1024 only when needed. A larger AOI or more hyperspectral channels increases memory and time. If an all-band budget is exceeded, reduce the AOI or grid: anomaly processing does not silently discard spectral bands.</p>
        <h3>Hyperspectral inputs</h3><p>RF, SVM, XGBoost and the trained U-Net can use mapped hyperspectral channels. Foundation and native pretrained heads have specific sensor/band requirements. The anomaly pipeline uses all connected science channels and reports excluded quality/display/atmospheric channels and constant channels. Check the saved band schema and source provenance rather than assuming every channel in an archive is a measured wavelength.</p>
        <p>Use <a href="#alignment" data-help-topic="alignment">band coregistration</a> for inter-band displacement. Sharing a CRS and pixel grid does not itself prove optical band alignment.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'models', group: 'Classify', title: 'Choose a LULC model',
      summary: 'Know which models are pretrained, which need references, and which can be combined.',
      keywords: 'algorithm Random Forest SVM XGBoost U-Net Prithvi Dynamic World OpenEarthMap pretrained foundation ensemble taxonomy classes',
      html: `<h3>Two starting paths</h3><p><strong>Native pretrained heads</strong> infer their released classes without local fitting, provided the input sensor, product, bands and resolution are compatible. <strong>Job-trained models</strong> learn your local class names from references. Both produce pixel maps; a model's presence in the list does not prove that its dependencies and weights are already installed or cached.</p>
        <table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Inputs & starting point</th><th>Training behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Random Forest</td><td>Mapped spectral channels; useful local baseline.</td><td>Refits from reviewed classes.</td></tr><tr><td>SVM</td><td>High-dimensional spectra and smaller labeled sets.</td><td>Refits from reviewed classes.</td></tr><tr><td>XGBoost</td><td>Nonlinear spectral relationships.</td><td>Refits from reviewed classes.</td></tr><tr><td>U-Net</td><td>Spatial context; enough labeled pixels for local classes.</td><td>Trains, or continues a compatible checkpoint.</td></tr><tr><td>Prithvi-EO-2.0</td><td>Pretrained encoder; six mapped reflectance bands including narrow NIR and SWIR.</td><td>Frozen encoder with a supervised classifier head. Not full encoder fine-tuning.</td></tr><tr><td>LULC Ensemble</td><td>Compatible trained RF/SVM/XGBoost/U-Net/Prithvi members; at least two successful members.</td><td>Refits members; combines compatible probabilities equally.</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamic World</td><td>Native 9-class head; Sentinel-2 L1C, 9 bands, native 10 m support.</td><td>Released-head inference; compatible checkpoint fine-tuning is available.</td></tr><tr><td>Prithvi crop/LULC</td><td>Native 13-class head; HLS 30 m, three real dates, 18 bands.</td><td>Released-head inference; compatible temporal-input fine-tuning.</td></tr><tr><td>OpenEarthMap</td><td>Native 8-class U-Net; RGB at 0.25–0.5 m.</td><td>Released-head inference; compatible checkpoint fine-tuning.</td></tr></tbody></table>
        <div class="guide-callout"><strong>One native pretrained taxonomy per run</strong><p>Run Dynamic World, Prithvi crop/LULC or OpenEarthMap separately. The worker rejects mixing a native pretrained head with other models in the same run. Their different classes cannot be averaged into the local trained ensemble without an explicit compatible crosswalk.</p></div>
        <h3>Which classes will I see?</h3><p>Job-trained models use the job's training class names. Check spelling and granularity before labeling; <i>Forest</i> and <i>Trees</i> are different labels if entered separately.</p><details><summary>Dynamic World · 9 classes</summary><p>Water; Trees; Grass; Flooded vegetation; Crops; Shrub and scrub; Built-up; Bare ground; Snow and ice.</p></details><details><summary>Prithvi crop/LULC · 13 classes</summary><p>Natural Vegetation; Forest; Corn; Soybeans; Wetlands; Developed/Barren; Open Water; Winter Wheat; Alfalfa; Fallow/Idle Cropland; Cotton; Sorghum; Other.</p></details><details><summary>OpenEarthMap · 8 classes</summary><p>Bareland; Rangeland; Developed space; Road; Tree; Water; Agriculture; Building.</p></details>
        <p>SAM proposes object boundaries and is not a semantic LULC voter. Anomaly models score unusual spectra and use normal/anomaly reviews, not these semantic classes.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'running', group: 'Classify', title: 'Run analysis & follow progress',
      summary: 'Prepare a plan, understand combination modes and monitor actual background work.',
      keywords: 'run inference progress bar queued running failed complete interrupted adaptive separate consensus grid worker slow',
      html: `<h3>Submit a run</h3><ol><li>Open <b>Manual inference</b> and choose the job and images. Use <b>Connect source pixels</b> if a source is missing.</li><li>Choose a compatible released or qualified model. Model cards identify the sensor, required bands and product level.</li><li>Set the analysis grid and any model-specific inputs. For SAM, draw its prompts on the image or choose a map box. Choose <b>Run selected</b>.</li><li>Follow the progress in <b>Manual inference</b> or <b>Activity &amp; Logs</b>. Open <b>All runs &amp; results</b> to see other mission runs.</li></ol>
        <h3>Pause, resume and stop</h3><p>Use the run controls beside its progress when available. Changes take effect at safe checkpoints. An interrupted execution may need to be resumed or restarted. Inspect saved outputs and errors before submitting a replacement. The percent indicator reports completed stages rather than minutes.</p>
        <h3>Read combined results</h3><p>Saved legacy reports may show adaptive, separate or consensus modes. Read each model's status and the recorded combination policy before interpreting agreement. The <b>LULC Ensemble</b> is a versioned classifier that combines compatible member predictions. It is not independent validation.</p>
        <p>If a required local model pack is missing, the run reports the missing component. Check <b>Algorithms &amp; published results</b> before choosing an adapter.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'reports', group: 'Inspect', title: 'Reports, classes & downloads',
      summary: 'Find outputs, compare agreement, and export geospatial evidence.',
      keywords: 'report outcome result output download JSON GeoTIFF confidence agreement disagreement classes percentages map legend',
      html: `<ol><li>Open <b>Mission workspace</b>, choose the mission and select <b>Data &amp; results → Runs &amp; results</b>.</li><li>Locate the run by job, time and state. Wait for completion, or expand errors for a failed model.</li><li>Review model identity, input provenance, selected combination, band alignment, class legend and evaluation scope.</li><li>Use the map/review action on the image output, or download its artifacts.</li></ol>
        <h3>Read the map layers</h3><ul><li><strong>Land-cover/consensus:</strong> the final class decision under the reported combination policy.</li><li><strong>Individual model maps:</strong> what each successful worker predicted.</li><li><strong>Disagreement:</strong> pixels where successful model predictions differ; orange highlights locations worth inspecting.</li><li><strong>Agreement:</strong> pixels where the compared predictions match. This does not prove the class is correct.</li><li><strong>Confidence:</strong> a model/combination score. It is not automatically a calibrated real-world probability or measured accuracy.</li><li><strong>No-data:</strong> unavailable or invalid analysis pixels; they are not a land-cover class.</li></ul>
        <p>The class breakdown lists names, counts and area shares on the analysis grid. A class can be part of the taxonomy but absent from this AOI. With only one successful model, there is no independent multi-model agreement evidence even if the disagreement map is empty.</p>
        <h3>What to save</h3><p>Download the full <b>JSON report</b> for provenance, model choices, metrics and errors; <b>GeoTIFFs</b> for GIS analysis; and <b>PNG previews</b> for quick visual comparison. Review-history and corrected products remain separate from the original predictions. Do not infer class IDs from display colors: consult the report taxonomy and raster metadata.</p>
        <p>Outputs use the reported resampled analysis grid. They do not replace the full-resolution source files. Metrics are meaningful only for the reference scope named in the report; see <a href="#metrics" data-help-topic="metrics">Precision, Recall & F1</a>.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'review', group: 'Inspect', title: 'Inspect votes & correct a result',
      summary: 'See who predicted what and explicitly save your own classification.',
      keywords: 'human review manually classify correct pixel votes who voted disagreement agreement inspector geometry',
      html: `<ol><li>From a completed inference output choose <b>Open image & classification</b> or <b>Open image & agreement</b>.</li><li>Click a valid analysis pixel, or enter its longitude/latitude in the inspector. The vote table lists each model's class, the original final class and your reviewed class where available.</li><li>Choose a model's <b>Use class</b> action or choose your own class in <b>Your classification</b>. Accepting a model's class does not by itself save a correction.</li><li>Select <b>Use clicked pixel</b> for a pixel reference, or choose <b>Draw image region</b> and drag a rectangle on the image. Confirm the geometry status.</li><li>Choose <b>Training / future fine-tuning</b> or <b>Independent validation only</b>, add a useful note and choose <b>Save my classification</b>.</li></ol>
        <p>You can inspect agreement pixels as well as disagreement pixels. If all algorithms agree on a wrong answer, it still needs correction. No-data pixels cannot provide a valid classification reference.</p>
        <h3>What gets saved?</h3><p>The saved review retains the source run, job/image scope, geometry and approved class. Reviewed products and reference records are stored locally while original model predictions remain intact. Saved references feed readiness checks; an algorithm is relevant only where its own prediction conflicts with the approved class.</p>
        <p>Keep independent validation regions separate from fitting data. Overlapping later decisions own the affected pixels; repeatedly drawing the same correction does not multiply unique evidence. Check the source date and land-cover definition before overriding the map.</p>
        <p>For more precise object boundaries, use <a href="#segmentation" data-help-topic="segmentation">SAM segmentation proposals</a> in the same inspector. A proposal is not ground truth until you assign a class and explicitly save it.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'segmentation', group: 'Inspect', title: 'SAM object segmentation',
      summary: 'Prompt candidate boundaries, compare masks and approve a labeled region.',
      keywords: 'SAM SAM2 segmentation mask include exclude point box boundary proposal quality composite',
      html: `<p><b>SAM 3 Tracker</b> proposes object boundaries. It does not identify land-cover classes, vote in the LULC ensemble or train automatically in the current app.</p>
        <p>To segment a source image before classification, choose the released SAM model in <b>Manual inference</b> and use <b>Draw SAM prompts on chosen image</b>. To segment during review, follow these steps.</p><ol><li>Open a completed classification result in the image comparison viewer. Locate <b>SAM 3 interactive segmentation</b> in the inspector.</li><li>Check <b>Display bands</b>, normally <code>RED,GREEN,BLUE</code>. These must exist in the connected raster. This is a three-band display composite; other spectral channels remain available to classifiers.</li><li>Click inside the target object and choose <b>Include clicked point</b>. Add exclude points to reject neighboring regions if needed, then choose <b>Segment points</b>. At most 32 prompt points are accepted.</li><li>Alternatively choose <b>Draw image region</b>, select a rectangle and choose <b>Segment selected region</b>. A polygon supplies its enclosing box, not an already accepted final boundary.</li><li>Watch segmentation progress. Compare the proposed masks and previews; use <b>Preview on map</b> or download GeoTIFF/GeoJSON.</li><li>Choose <b>Use this boundary</b>, assign a semantic class in the review form, set its reference role and choose <b>Save my classification</b>.</li></ol>
        <p>Quality shown on a proposal is the model's own mask estimate, not independently measured Precision, Recall or F1. History retains prompts, display bands, geometry, model provenance and candidate outputs. Unapproved masks never become references.</p>
        <p>The source window is read on a bounded, georeferenced grid, up to 1024 pixels on the longest side. Inspect thin or small features at that resolution. SAM fine-tuning is unsupported until a supervised mask-training adapter exists; approved masks can instead support the semantic classifier correction workflow.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'anomalies', group: 'Detect anomalies', title: 'Full-spectrum anomaly detection',
      summary: 'Run four detectors, adjust sensitivity and label unusual spectra.',
      keywords: 'anomaly RX local RX Isolation Forest autoencoder percentile threshold votes normal background full spectrum epochs guard outer window',
      html: `<h3>Choose imagery and detectors</h3><p>Open a mission's <b>Spectral anomalies</b> tab. Select a job and images with ready raster access. Choose <b>Regularized RX</b>, <b>Local RX</b>, <b>Isolation Forest</b> and/or <b>Spectral autoencoder</b>. They use all connected science bands; exclusions and constant channels are reported explicitly.</p>
        <table><thead><tr><th>Detector</th><th>What it compares</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Regularized RX</td><td>Spectral distance from a regularized global background.</td></tr><tr><td>Local RX</td><td>A local outer/guard-ring background. The implementation shares covariance within 16 × 16 score cells and reports fallback use.</td></tr><tr><td>Isolation Forest</td><td>Nonlinear isolation of unusual spectral feature combinations.</td></tr><tr><td>Spectral autoencoder</td><td>Reconstruction error from learned background spectra. This is a spectral model, not a spatial/convolutional autoencoder.</td></tr></tbody></table>
        <ol><li>Start with <b>Unreviewed scene background</b> when you have no approved normal references. Scene background may contain genuine anomalies.</li><li>Set a grid and percentile; the initial percentile is 99.5. Choose a minimum vote count no greater than the number of enabled detectors.</li><li>Expand <b>Local windows & autoencoder training</b> if needed. Outer and guard windows must be odd; the outer window must exceed the guard window. Pixels here refer to the analysis grid. More epochs can take longer.</li><li>Choose <b>Run spectral anomaly detection</b> and watch progress and saved outputs.</li></ol>
        <h3>Understand sensitivity</h3><p>A percentile of 99.5 selects approximately the top 0.5% of scores per detector, subject to ties. A lower percentile flags more pixels; a higher one flags fewer. Requiring more votes is generally more selective. Percentiles are scene-relative ranks, not probabilities. High scores can indicate real targets, clouds, shadows, bad calibration or sensor artifacts.</p>
        <h3>Review and adapt</h3><p>Inspect individual heatmaps and combined votes. Change percentile or minimum votes and choose <b>Apply threshold · save new view</b>; this saves another threshold interpretation of existing scores rather than retraining detectors. Choose <b>Inspect & review on map</b>, select a pixel or drawn AOI, label it <b>Confirmed anomaly</b> or <b>Normal background / false alarm</b>, choose confidence and reference role, then save.</p>
        <p>Approved normal training pixels can support a separate reviewed-background candidate; confirmed anomalies and validation references are excluded from background fitting. Benchmark validation/test images are protected. Autoencoder reconstruction holdout loss is not anomaly accuracy. Precision/Recall/F1 require human validation references and carry their sampling limitations.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'ground-truth', group: 'Learn', title: 'Create trustworthy references',
      summary: 'Save reviewed map geometry with a class, role, confidence and provenance.',
      keywords: 'ground truth labels references train validation confidence provenance class polygons points',
      html: `<ol><li>Draw the reference region in the map workspace and finish the geometry.</li><li>Open the mission's <b>Data &amp; results → Label data</b> tab and select the correct job.</li><li>Enter the <b>Land-cover class</b>. Use consistent names across this job.</li><li>Choose <b>Training reference</b> to permit fitting, or <b>Independent validation</b> to exclude it from fitting.</li><li>Choose your confidence (1 tentative to 5 certain) and add a source/date or interpretation note.</li><li>Choose <b>Save current map AOI as ground truth</b>. Confirm the saved reference appears for that job.</li></ol>
        <p>The geometry, mission/job scope, role, confidence and provenance are retained locally. This form creates job-level references; image-specific corrections can be made from a run inspector, or an image scope selected during vector import. A geometry outside valid raster coverage does not create useful training pixels.</p>
        <h3>Reference quality</h3><p>Use surveyed or independently interpreted labels where possible. Label homogeneous regions and avoid uncertain boundaries, cloud contamination and outdated land use. A confidently entered class can still be wrong; confidence is your assessment, not a model score.</p>
        <p>Keep validation samples spatially and, preferably, temporally independent. A label used for fitting cannot also provide independent proof of accuracy. Point references provide little pixel support on coarse grids; check counts and support in the report.</p>
        <div class="guide-callout"><strong>Be deliberate about uncertain training labels</strong><p>Semantic fitting requires confidence of at least 3/5. Use independent evidence to choose the validation role. A training confidence score describes your review, not measured accuracy.</p></div>
        <p>To import external reference polygons see <a href="#vectors" data-help-topic="vectors">shapefile import</a>. To understand model versions and thresholds see <a href="#training" data-help-topic="training">fine-tuning & automatic learning</a>.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'vectors', group: 'Learn', title: 'Import polygon shapefiles',
      summary: 'Read external polygons, check projection and map attributes to class labels.',
      keywords: 'shapefile shp shx dbf prj cpg zip vector polygons import external CRS EPSG encoding attribute class mapping',
      html: `<h3>Prepare the files</h3><p>Use a ZIP containing matching <code>.shp</code>, <code>.shx</code> and <code>.dbf</code> components, ideally with <code>.prj</code> and optional <code>.cpg</code>. You can also select the matching components together. The importer expects polygon geometry, not arbitrary vector formats or an unlabeled raster.</p>
        <ol><li>Open <b>Data &amp; results → Label data → Import polygon shapefile</b> and choose the target job.</li><li>Select the files. If projection is missing, enter the actual source CRS, such as <code>EPSG:32636</code>, in <b>Source CRS override</b>. Do not guess EPSG:4326 for projected coordinates.</li><li>Choose text encoding if automatic interpretation is wrong, then <b>Read polygons & attributes</b>. If a ZIP has multiple datasets, choose one and read again.</li><li>Check polygon count, invalid-feature messages and the coordinate transformation. Use <b>Preview polygons on map</b> to verify location.</li><li>Select a <b>Class attribute</b> and map each value to the class name you want, or enter <b>One class for all polygons</b>.</li><li>Choose <b>Image scope</b>, reference role, confidence and a source note. Explicitly choose whether to skip unlabeled polygons.</li><li>Choose <b>Import references into job</b>. Inspect the imported/skipped summary.</li></ol>
        <p>Reading and previewing do not approve ground truth. The import/commit step saves your explicit class mapping and role. Original source files, checksums, attributes and normalized WGS84 geometries are retained for provenance. Imported training references participate in the saved correction policy; validation references are excluded from fitting.</p>
        <h3>Limits and common problems</h3><p>The importer limits uploads to 20 components and 64 MiB combined input, ZIPs to 1,000 entries and 256 MiB declared expanded data, and datasets to 10,000 polygons and 2 million vertices. The class-mapping UI supports up to 200 distinct values for the selected field. Choose a categorical class field instead of a unique polygon ID. Split large datasets or simplify excessive geometry before import.</p>
        <p>Missing components, duplicate components, wrong encoding, invalid geometry or an incorrect CRS must be resolved at the source. Keep class definitions compatible with the job or pretrained model you intend to train.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'training', group: 'Learn', title: 'Fine-tuning & automatic learning',
      summary: 'Understand readiness, separate model versions and how corrections trigger training.',
      keywords: 'retrain training finetune fine-tuning automatic rule 20 500 2 confidence threshold candidate checkpoint version frozen encoder',
      html: `<h3>Manual training</h3><p>In <b>Training &amp; qualification</b>, use <b>Add model configuration</b> and choose the training job, algorithm, classes and qualification rule. Connect the source pixels and choose <b>Label / import data</b>. Use <b>Check sample coverage</b> to check each class, then choose <b>Train a candidate</b> or the model’s fine-tuning action. Review progress, validation and errors in the same workspace.</p>
        <p>RF/SVM/XGBoost refit; U-Net can continue a compatible checkpoint; Prithvi trains a downstream head with its foundation encoder frozen; the ensemble refits compatible members; native pretrained heads require their own compatible data/taxonomy for fine-tuning. SAM training is not implemented. The anomaly models use reviewed-background refits rather than semantic class training.</p>
        <h3>The saved automatic policy</h3><p>The approved default is <strong>20 distinct relevant reviewed regions, 500 unique corrected analysis pixels and 2 images per relevant algorithm</strong>, with confidence at least 3/5. The displayed persisted policy is authoritative; saved settings survive restarts. Thresholds apply per algorithm, not to all mission annotations pooled indiscriminately.</p>
        <ul><li>A correction is relevant where that algorithm's saved prediction differs from your approved answer.</li><li>Overlapping edits are deduplicated; the latest decision owns each pixel.</li><li>Validation/test references and held-out pixels do not count as fitting evidence.</li><li>Each eligible algorithm gets its own separate, versioned candidate run.</li><li>Evidence is reserved to prevent duplicate/concurrent batches, and consumed only after successful completion.</li><li>Failures remain inspectable; the same failed batch is not automatically repeated without new evidence.</li></ul>
        <p>The app checks readiness after saved corrections and related state changes, and at service startup. It announces when automatic training starts; no additional confirmation is requested. The backend must be running. Counts below the saved thresholds are a normal waiting state, not a failed training job.</p>
        <h3>New versions do not silently replace established models</h3><p>Keep the original prediction and report, inspect the candidate's training provenance and independent metrics, and compare the same reference scope. Candidates can seed later compatible training. Ordinary inference does not automatically adopt a training candidate. Review the independent metrics and use <b>Qualify &amp; move to inference</b> when the rule is met. Use the model’s return-to-training action when you need to revise it. Operational pipelines require a qualified version.</p>
        <p>Reference roles and the minimum confidence are described under <a href="#ground-truth" data-help-topic="ground-truth">reference quality</a>. Add accurate training labels rather than trying to satisfy counts with repeated or uncertain edits.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'alignment', group: 'Prepare imagery', title: 'Align bands within an image',
      summary: 'Use automatic grid-level coregistration or export an aligned local GeoTIFF.',
      keywords: 'band alignment coregistration registration phase correlation reference NIR offset shift scene',
      html: `<h3>Automatic alignment during classification</h3><p>When a run enables band coregistration, it applies on that run’s analysis grid. The worker compares image structure to a reference, normally NIR when available, and applies translations only when quality, shift-size and improvement checks pass. The report records estimated shifts and whether each was applied. A skipped shift does not mean a processing failure.</p>
        <h3>Standalone multiband GeoTIFF tool</h3><ol><li>Open <b>Data &amp; results → Label data → Band coregistration</b>.</li><li>Choose a georeferenced multiband GeoTIFF. It needs at least two bands.</li><li>Set <b>Reference band</b> using its one-based channel index.</li><li>Choose <b>Align bands & save GeoTIFF</b>. Read the reported x/y shifts and download the aligned GeoTIFF.</li><li>Inspect the result, put it inside the local raster folder if needed, and explicitly reconnect it through <b>Data &amp; results → Source data</b> for subsequent analysis. Producing the file does not automatically replace a job's source.</li></ol>
        <div class="guide-callout"><strong>Band alignment, not scene alignment</strong><p>This corrects translation between channels of one acquisition. It is not multi-date scene registration, orthorectification, terrain correction or a guarantee that different sensors align. Large rotation, distortion or parallax needs a different preprocessing method.</p></div>
        <p>Phase correlation works best when shared structure is visible in the reference and moving bands. Spectrally different features, clouds and no-data can mislead it. The standalone tool decodes the full stack and can be expensive; use manageable files or prepare a smaller source externally. Keep the original raster and verify borders/validity after shifts.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'metrics', group: 'Reference', title: 'Precision, Recall, F1 & evaluation',
      summary: 'Interpret scores, class support and validation scope without confusing agreement with accuracy.',
      keywords: 'precision recall F1 macro accuracy IoU confusion matrix support test benchmark validation holdout confidence agreement',
      html: `<table><thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Question answered</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Precision</td><td>Of the pixels predicted as a class, how many match the reference? TP / (TP + FP).</td></tr><tr><td>Recall</td><td>Of the reference pixels belonging to a class, how many were found? TP / (TP + FN).</td></tr><tr><td>F1</td><td>The harmonic mean of Precision and Recall; useful when both false positives and missed detections matter.</td></tr><tr><td>Macro average</td><td>Gives each evaluated class equal weight. Read per-class scores and support alongside it.</td></tr><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>Fraction of evaluated reference pixels classified correctly. A dominant class can hide weak minority classes.</td></tr><tr><td>IoU</td><td>Class overlap: intersection divided by union. Mean IoU summarizes class overlap.</td></tr><tr><td>Support</td><td>How much reference evidence contributed to a class score.</td></tr></tbody></table>
        <h3>Always read the scope</h3><p>Explicit validation geometries are excluded from training. When explicit validation is absent, trained workflows can use an internal eastern spatial holdout with a buffer; the report identifies this. That is weaker evidence than independent scenes or dates. A model chosen using validation should be assessed separately on untouched test data.</p>
        <p>A blank or unmeasured metric means the required reference support is unavailable. It does not mean zero performance or perfect performance. Per-class values with little support can be unstable. Human-review sampling often overrepresents difficult pixels; anomaly-review metrics need that selection-bias caveat.</p>
        <h3>Agreement is not validation</h3><p>Several models can share the same bias. Detector vote counts, anomaly percentiles, confidence values and SAM mask quality are not measured accuracy. Evaluate against independent references. For anomalies, normal and anomaly references are a different taxonomy from semantic LULC classes.</p>
        <h3>Benchmark reports</h3><p>Existing benchmark missions, if present, appear in <b>Mission workspace → Data &amp; results → Runs &amp; results</b>. Read the dataset, region, class crosswalk, spatial grid, train/validation/test split and sampling protocol. A regional pilot or binary water test is not a global multiclass leaderboard result. Frozen inputs and original references are retained for reuse; official validation/test imagery must not enter fitting.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'storage', group: 'Reference', title: 'Start, save, back up & recover',
      summary: 'Know what persists locally, what requires the backend, and how to protect your project.',
      keywords: 'install startup Docker localhost save database SQLite backup restore cache disk restart interrupted offline multiuser security',
      html: `<h3>Start and stop</h3><p>With Docker Desktop and Docker Compose installed, an operator runs <code>docker compose up --build</code> from the project directory, then opens <code>http://localhost:8080</code>. MC² is normally on port 8000. Use the served application, not <code>file://…/static/index.html</code>; application API calls require the backend. The standalone manual can be read without the backend.</p><p><code>docker compose down</code> stops the supplied services without intentionally deleting the mounted project folders. Rebuilding after a code change is different from deleting saved data.</p>
        <h3>Persistent project folders</h3><table><thead><tr><th>Location under data/</th><th>Contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>spectrascope/spectrascope.db</td><td>Missions, jobs, references, run records, policy, evidence and result indexes.</td></tr><tr><td>spectrascope/local-rasters/</td><td>Your local source stacks.</td></tr><tr><td>spectrascope/raster-cache/</td><td>Complete downloaded science assets.</td></tr><tr><td>spectrascope/results/</td><td>Prediction products, reports and model versions.</td></tr><tr><td>spectrascope/segmentations/</td><td>Segmentation runs and masks.</td></tr><tr><td>spectrascope/benchmarks/frozen/</td><td>Reusable benchmark snapshots and provenance.</td></tr><tr><td>mapcontrol/</td><td>MC² map database and generated files.</td></tr></tbody></table>
        <p>Other analysis assets, anomaly runs, vector imports and model caches also live under <code>data/spectrascope/</code>. Keep that complete folder rather than backing up only the database. Source paths may be required to reopen or reproduce a run; a JSON report is not a copy of its rasters or weights.</p>
        <h3>Back up safely</h3><ol><li>Wait for active work to finish, then stop both services for a consistent copy.</li><li>Copy the full <code>data/spectrascope/</code> and <code>data/mapcontrol/</code> directories to a separate backup location. Keep the code/configuration needed to reproduce the environment.</li><li>To restore, stop services and restore a matching database/artifact set with the same relative source paths before restarting. Do not overwrite a running SQLite database with an unrelated snapshot.</li></ol>
        <h3>Availability and access</h3><p>Public catalog search and remote raster streaming require network access. Strict offline mode uses local imagery, bundled maps and installed model packs. This is a shared local workspace, not an established per-user isolation system. Network exposure and authentication depend on operator configuration; the source audit did not certify a secure multi-user deployment.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'troubleshooting', group: 'Reference', title: 'Troubleshooting & common questions',
      summary: 'Find the next useful action when search, access, models or review do not behave as expected.',
      keywords: 'error failed blank empty missing stuck unavailable timeout slow cannot not enough 401 403 404 invalid CRS restart help FAQ',
      html: `<table><thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody>
        <tr><td>Map service unavailable</td><td>Use localhost:8080 rather than opening index.html as a file. Confirm the app and MC² services are running, then refresh. An operator should check service logs and internal/public MC² URLs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No catalog results or gaps</td><td>Complete/read the AOI, widen dates, loosen cloud cover, check sensor history and load remaining pages. Provider limits and real acquisition footprints affect coverage.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Cannot see the result table</td><td>Use Results on a small viewport. Close an active map inspector with Return to catalog results. The map and table are side by side on larger displays.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Preview uploaded but cannot run analysis</td><td>Preview upload only displays a raster. Create a mission/job image entry and configure analysis raster access separately.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Local path rejected/not found</td><td>Put the file beneath the server's configured local raster root and use its relative path. Check the Docker mount, spelling, suffix and permissions; a browser file path is not a server path.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Remote asset 401/403/timeout</td><td>Metadata access and raster access can differ. Ask the operator to configure supported provider authentication or download an authorized copy externally and connect it locally. Repeated clicks do not bypass access policy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Raster has no CRS / AOI does not intersect</td><td>Provide genuinely georeferenced data; check coordinates, source CRS and footprint. Renaming a file or declaring a different product level does not georeference it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Missing or incompatible bands</td><td>Inspect the actual channels, band order, product level and model requirements. L1C is not L2A; B8 is not B8A; one HLS date is not three.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Native pretrained models cannot run together</td><td>Choose one native head per run. Run different taxonomies separately. Use the trained ensemble only for compatible shared classes and inputs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Add at least two classes / insufficient training pixels</td><td>Create reliable training-role references for at least two classes that intersect valid pixels. Check image scope, class spelling and the model's required pixel support. Native-head inference can instead run without local fitting.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Model weights/dependency unavailable</td><td>Read the worker error. Use the matching offline component for this model. Check the availability message in Manual inference. A pretrained label describes the model’s starting weights.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Queued or seemingly stuck progress</td><td>Read status/stage and whether another job is active. Work can wait for a shared RAM or accelerator slot. Keep the backend running; inspect errors and operator logs before resubmitting to avoid duplicate work.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Memory budget / all-band cube too large</td><td>Reduce AOI or analysis-grid size. Preserve science channels and inspect the exclusions list. Avoid full-scene/high-band standalone alignment when memory is limited.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>All models agree but result looks wrong</td><td>Inspect individual votes, source calibration, classes and actual pixels. Save an independent human correction; agreement alone is not evidence of correctness.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Metrics are unmeasured or blank</td><td>Add independent validation references with adequate support. Model confidence, SAM quality and autoencoder reconstruction loss do not substitute for labeled validation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Automatic training has not started</td><td>Check the saved rule and per-algorithm counts, distinct images, relevant wrong predictions and confidence. Overlapping edits count once; validation and held-out data do not count. A failed evidence batch waits for new evidence.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>New trained model did not replace old inference</td><td>Candidates are deliberately separate. Review their reports; ordinary inference does not silently adopt candidates. There is no general promotion button in this version.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Anomaly scores seem extreme</td><td>Check bands, no-data, calibration, clouds and background choice. Percentiles are relative to the scene. Change percentile/vote count and save a new view before deciding what is truly anomalous.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shapefile is in the wrong place or labels look garbled</td><td>Use the actual source CRS and correct .prj; choose the right encoding/.cpg. Confirm matching components and preview before committing references.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SAM mask or band shifts look wrong</td><td>Check display/reference bands, prompts, source validity and grid. Add exclude points, use a tighter box, or reject the proposal. An estimated quality/shift score is not independent validation.</td></tr>
        </tbody></table><h3>When asking the operator for help</h3><p>Include the mission/job, run ID, image ID, model, access mode, grid, error text and whether another job is active. Export the report if available. Share sanitized configuration and logs; do not include credentials or private imagery unnecessarily.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'basemaps', group: 'Maps & sources', title: 'Your map services and image basemaps',
      summary: 'Connect WMS, WMTS, ArcGIS and tile services, or add your own georeferenced display image.',
      keywords: 'basemap mapping service wms wmts arcgis mapserver xyz tilejson maplibre own custom url tiles imagery',
      html: `<p>Choose <b>＋ Your maps</b> above the discovery map. Give the provider a name, choose its type and enter your service URL. Configuration and switching use MC² MCP tools. Saved definitions live in the local application database and the map configuration.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Required settings</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WMS</td><td>Endpoint, layer name(s), optional style and version 1.3.0 or 1.1.1. The server must support EPSG:3857. The viewer requests Web Mercator GetMap tiles.</td></tr><tr><td>WMTS KVP</td><td>Endpoint, layer, style and a Web Mercator tile matrix set. Numeric zoom IDs are required; a prefix such as EPSG:3857: is supported. Non-Web-Mercator or irregular matrix grids need a compatible tiling service.</td></tr><tr><td>ArcGIS cached</td><td>A MapServer endpoint; requests its tile/z/y/x path. Use a Web Mercator tile cache.</td></tr><tr><td>ArcGIS dynamic</td><td>A MapServer endpoint; requests Web Mercator export images. Optional comma-separated layer IDs choose visible sublayers.</td></tr><tr><td>XYZ / TileJSON / MapLibre style</td><td>XYZ templates contain {z}, {x} and {y}; the others point to their JSON document. All dependent resources must be accessible to the browser.</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Save &amp; use service</b> registers the provider and reloads the map to load its definition. Finish an active drawing first; unfinished sketches cannot survive a reload. Persisted AOIs, overlays and the saved camera are restored. A saved provider can be reused or removed. Existing providers can be replaced by saving a new definition, then removing the old one. Up to 40 custom providers are allowed.</p><p>Tiles are fetched directly by the browser. Check service CORS, reachability, HTTPS and authentication if the basemap is blank. The app does not proxy arbitrary service URLs or discover capabilities. Enter the exact layer/style/matrix identifiers from your provider. Embedded username/password URLs are rejected. A query-string token is stored with the URL and visible to local app users; use a scoped map token.</p><h3>Your own image as a basemap</h3><p>Use the GeoTIFF form in the same dialog and select one grayscale band or three RGB bands by one-based index. The file must be georeferenced, up to 64 MiB, 64 million source pixels and 512 bands. A display preview up to 1024 pixels per side is reprojected and stretched before storage. The bounds must fit Web Mercator and not cross the antimeridian. This is a background preview, not an analysis raster or a full-resolution tiled image service. For larger or detailed backgrounds, use your own WMS/WMTS/ArcGIS/XYZ service. Science bands and raw source pixels remain part of the separate raster-access workflow.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'runtime', group: 'Setup & storage', title: 'Windows, memory profiles and dedicated GPUs',
      summary: 'Configure a connected Docker deployment or use the offline native desktop/browser installers.',
      keywords: 'windows 16 gb ram memory machine hardware gpu nvidia cuda cpu ollama local automatic profiles docker wsl powershell desktop offline',
      html: `<p>The Windows Current User offline wizard supplies the browser engine and local runtimes without administrator rights, Docker or WSL. The 0.2.25 media has been inspected and is awaiting native Windows verification. The connected Docker profile below is a separate option.</p><h3>Windows with 16 GB RAM</h3><ol><li>Install Docker Desktop with its WSL2 backend and Ollama for Windows. Keep sufficient free disk space for model weights, Docker images and science rasters.</li><li>From the project directory in PowerShell, run <code>./scripts/start-windows.ps1 -PullModel -Start</code>. The script detects host RAM and dedicated GPUs, downloads only the selected model, writes host capability/default files, then starts the application. Existing saved in-app model settings take precedence. If script execution is restricted by your organization, follow its approved execution policy; the script does not change that policy.</li><li>For 16 GB hosts, the optional compose override caps the app at 6 GiB and map service at 1 GiB, limits numerical worker threads, and starts inference with a 192-pixel analysis grid. These are resource limits, not a guarantee that every model/training workload fits. An oversized workload may fail or be stopped by the container limit; use smaller grids, images and batches.</li><li>Open <b>Insights → LLM connection</b>. Automatic model selection uses the host detection file, avoiding the mistake of treating the Docker VM memory as total machine RAM. The Compact profile chooses Qwen3.5 2B with a 4K context and unloads it after each reply.</li></ol><h3>Other hosts and manual choices</h3><p>On Mac/Linux or a host with Python, run <code>python scripts/configure_local_llm.py --pull</code> before Docker starts. Without --pull it records detection and defaults only. In-app settings offer Automatic, Compact (16 GB / 2B), Balanced (24–32 GB / 4B), Higher quality (48 GB+ / 9B), or your own installed model. Automatic remains conservative and chooses 4B even on a 48 GB host for simple planning. Missing models are not silently substituted or downloaded by the web server; download them through Ollama or the host setup script.</p><h3>GPU rule</h3><p>A detected, supported dedicated GPU may accelerate the LLM and supported TensorFlow/PyTorch inference or training adapters. Otherwise they use CPU. Supported native Apple Silicon inference can use MPS. Docker on macOS runs these models on the CPU unless they are handed off to a native worker. Windows setup enables the optional NVIDIA Docker passthrough override when it detects NVIDIA; working drivers, WSL2 and Docker GPU support are still required. AMD/Intel support depends on the actual runtime; detection alone does not establish support. GPU visibility inside the app container is checked independently. Rasterio, NumPy operations and classical CPU classifiers remain on CPU. Inspect the detected hardware and requested execution mode in LLM settings; this is distinct from a measured GPU benchmark.</p><h3>Offline scope</h3><p>Downloaded rasters, saved outputs, local operations and cached model weights can be used locally. Online catalogs and uncached remote basemaps need network access. Native offline installers already include map scripts, fonts, a local world basemap, runtimes and approved model packs. The connected Docker scripts are a separate deployment option; strict offline Windows installation does not require Docker, WSL or Hyper-V.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'insights', group: 'Reuse & insights', title: 'Saved layers, spatial operations and chat',
      summary: 'Reuse job outcomes across missions, approve an operation and export a georeferenced result.',
      keywords: 'insights layer library database save export catalog directory geotiff chat llm ollama intersection union mask threshold change cross classification opacity swipe',
      html: `<h3>Save results for later</h3><ol><li>Open <b>Insights &amp; layers</b> in the workspace header, mission tabs, or entry page. In Runs &amp; results, <b>Save / reuse result layers</b> opens the same studio.</li><li>Under <b>Job results</b>, find LULC class maps, individual classifier outputs, measured agreement/disagreement masks, confidence maps, anomaly scores/percentiles, threshold masks/votes and SAM mask proposals. Only finished runs are listed. SAM proposals remain proposals.</li><li>Choose <b>Save to library</b>. A new immutable GeoTIFF copy is written locally. SQLite records its hash, grid, class legend and source mission/job/run/image. Saved layers are available across all missions.</li></ol>
      <h3>Import and export</h3><p><b>Import</b> accepts a single-band, self-contained, georeferenced GeoTIFF up to 128 MiB and four million pixels. Choose continuous values, categorical class codes, or a binary 0/1 mask. Optionally provide a JSON class legend, for example <code>[{"value":1,"label":"Water","color":"#528aff"}]</code>. External mask, overview and auxiliary metadata files must be embedded into the TIFF first. RGB display composites are not analytical input layers.</p><p><b>Export…</b> offers a GeoTIFF download, a copy in the local catalog, and a directory picker when supported by the browser. The directory picker writes TIFF and metadata JSON; it does not silently overwrite an existing TIFF filename. With other browsers, use the download and browser Save as. The local catalog is <code>data/spectrascope/layer-catalog/</code> on the host (<code>/app/data/layer-catalog</code> in Docker). A catalog export includes a JSON sidecar with its legend and lineage. Import a catalog entry to reuse it as an input. Copying an arbitrary file into a server path does not automatically index it.</p>
      <h3>Describe, review, approve</h3><ol><li>In <b>Saved</b>, check one or two layers. The selection order assigns <b>A</b> and <b>B</b>. Remove and reselect to change the order.</li><li>Name the new output and type a request. Without an LLM, use one of the explicit command examples below. With a configured LLM, conversational requests are translated to the same supported operations. Clear supported commands bypass the LLM. Questions and clarification replies never schedule pixel processing; approved operations always use the local raster engine.</li><li>Choose <b>Interpret request</b>. Read the interpretation, exact input identities, units, scale/offset, reference grid, resampling and nodata policy. Nothing is queued until you choose <b>Approve &amp; create layer</b>. Discard a plan to abandon it, or submit a revised request to create a new plan.</li><li>Watch queued/running progress. A completed operation saves a new layer and loads it on the map. Failed runs keep their error and preserve all inputs. <b>Operation history</b> retains plans, approvals and results; running operations can be followed after reopening Insights. An application restart marks interrupted operations failed rather than pretending they completed.</li></ol>
      <table><thead><tr><th>Example command</th><th>Required inputs / result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>intersection of A and B / union of A and B</td><td>Two binary masks; true where both/either equal 1. Only valid overlap is evaluated, including union.</td></tr><tr><td>subtract B from A / mean of A and B</td><td>Two continuous layers. A minus B, or their arithmetic mean.</td></tr><tr><td>change map</td><td>Two categorical maps with identical recorded class legends. True where class codes differ.</td></tr><tr><td>cross-classify A and B</td><td>Two categorical maps; a new class and pair legend for each observed A/B pair, up to 4096 combinations.</td></tr><tr><td>mask A with B</td><td>Any A and binary B. Preserve A where B equals 1; elsewhere nodata.</td></tr><tr><td>A &gt;= 95</td><td>One continuous layer. Also supports &gt;, &lt;, &lt;=, == and !=.</td></tr><tr><td>extract class 6 from A</td><td>One categorical layer; a 0/1 mask for class code 6.</td></tr></tbody></table>
      <div class="guide-callout"><strong>Grid and scientific meaning</strong><p>A defines the full native output grid and CRS. B is reprojected to it with nearest-neighbor resampling for classes/masks or bilinear for continuous values. Areas outside valid coverage of any input become nodata. This grid reprojection does not estimate scene displacement or perform band alignment. Operations use stored numeric values; they do not apply scale/offset tags, convert units, or establish causality. Derived layers are not validation references or ground truth and never trigger retraining.</p></div>
      <h3>Use the managed local model</h3><p>Open <b>LLM connection</b>, select <b>Use managed Ollama</b>, then select an installed model and save. The launcher starts the model service and verifies the local process before private requests. Independent services cannot receive private requests without a verified network boundary. Explicit raster commands still run locally if managed chat is unavailable. Model selection never downloads a model.</p><p>The LLM receives your request and the selected layer names, types, units, scale/offset and class legends, not raster pixels. Its proposal must match a supported operation and pass type/schema validation before approval is offered. Unsupported multi-step operations require clarification. A vision model may be configured as a text planner if it supports the API, but this adapter does not send images or provide VLM visual review. Model weights are stored separately in Ollama. Connected host setup can explicitly download a starter model; the Docker application image does not bundle its weights. Offline installer media supplies the model packs locally. Change the selection in connection settings. The LLM is separate from all LULC/anomaly inference models. Automatic mode selects Qwen3.5 2B for a 16 GB host and 4B for hosts with at least 24 GB. A 9B quality profile is optional for 48 GB or more. Detection uses the host setup file when running in Docker; a container memory limit is not treated as physical host RAM. Supported local acceleration depends on the runtime and execution policy. Inspect the active hardware in the connection settings. Compact mode unloads the LLM after each reply. Manual model choices override automatic selection and may require more memory.</p>
      <h3>Opacity and swipe on every map</h3><p>Expand <b>Layers · Opacity &amp; swipe</b> in the map’s lower-left corner. Select any loaded layer and adjust opacity from 0 to 100 percent. Opacity is saved on that map asset. To compare, choose a left layer (or the underlying map) and start swipe. Move the divider slider and pan/zoom the synchronized views. Stop swipe before editing map layers. Swipe is temporary and closes when layers or map style change. These controls also work on earlier inference, anomaly and segmentation displays. Class maps have a categorical display legend; continuous maps use a display stretch. Exported TIFF pixel values are unaffected.</p>
      <h3>Local storage and backup</h3><p>Keep <code>spectrascope.db</code>, <code>layer-library/</code> and <code>layer-catalog/</code> together when backing up the local data folder. Database metadata alone does not contain raster pixels. Back up the private LLM key file separately only if required, with appropriate access restrictions. See the <a href="#storage" data-help-topic="storage">storage chapter</a>.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'mission-workspaces', group: 'Analysis', title: 'Pipelines, qualification and run controls',
      summary: 'Automatic pipelines, Training & qualification, Manual inference, and Activity & Logs.',
      keywords: 'workflow pipeline graph automatic mission qualification release logs pause resume stop classes detection monitoring',
      html: `<h3>One mission, three clear parts</h3><p>The mission-definition screen now has Automatic pipelines, Training &amp; qualification and Manual inference. Source data, existing results and the large result viewer stay available under Data &amp; results. Original mission/job/image records remain saved.</p><h3>Training &amp; qualification</h3><ol><li>Add a named model configuration and choose its training job, algorithm and classes. Released native heads keep their fixed taxonomies.</li><li>Choose validation goals or explicit operator verification. Operator verification is not a claim of measured local accuracy. Per-class goals can be set before training.</li><li>Label or import references. Reviewed Dynamic World outputs may seed a student classifier, with human corrections overriding teacher predictions. Teacher predictions never become validation or automatic training evidence.</li><li>Train a separate candidate, run a released pretrained verification, or select an existing completed candidate. Real progress, loss records where supplied, and independent Precision/Recall/F1 are shown. Batch estimators do not have invented epoch curves.</li><li>Review the evidence, tick the release confirmation and record a note. The backend checks the goals and checkpoint files before release.</li></ol><p>Qualified configurations disappear from the training list and appear in Manual inference. Improve / add classes returns that configuration to training and blocks dependent operational work. Existing results remain preserved. A changed class set for a fixed pretrained head requires a compatible student/head adapter; it cannot be accomplished by renaming native class IDs. Automatic correction-triggered training follows the saved rule and produces a candidate; it does not silently release the candidate.</p><h3>Manual inference</h3><p>Choose a job, check images and one or several qualified configurations, then Run selected. The operational worker uses pinned fitted checkpoints; it does not retrain from labels. Different class taxonomies run separately. Use All runs &amp; results for the original/result viewer, zoom, opacity, swipe, class legends and per-result exports.</p><p>For SAM, choose one image and Draw SAM prompts on chosen image. Click include/exclude points or draw a box on the original. SAM produces unclassified boundary proposals. Download masks/boundaries or explicitly assign and approve a class. Estimated mask quality is not measured accuracy. SAM training remains unsupported.</p><h3>Automatic pipelines</h3><ol><li>Create a pipeline/AOI. Choose source, sensors and task types. Draw or import an AOI. Each pipeline can monitor its own AOI.</li><li>Select Design process flow. Drag blocks to arrange them. Click an output circle followed by an input circle to connect them. Select a block to configure its model or output formats.</li><li>Use parallel classifier branches and explicit Compare blocks for compatible class schemas. A Class → enclosing box block can supply a downstream SAM prompt; also connect the science image input to SAM. One enclosing box is generated, not a collection of independently verified objects.</li><li>Validate and save. Choose whether to process existing images on first activation. Activate monitoring explicitly.</li></ol><p>Local folders keep their saved checkbox authority. Complete, settled multiband products enter the local catalog; catalog changes are checked for true geometric AOI intersection. Online catalog polling uses existing adapters when connected. No models or packages are downloaded at runtime. Duplicate discoveries for the same source revision and pipeline version do not create duplicate runs. Missing providers, folders, bands, calibration or qualifications remain visible in logs.</p><p>Processing branches are scheduled in parallel where resource limits permit. Conservative profiles use one heavy worker; extended host/container memory can permit two. Paused native work can retain its worker slot and in-memory state. Stop pending work if you need to release that slot. Monitoring requires local services to be running; it cannot operate while the workstation is off.</p><p>Exports are stored under workflow-exports with source hashes, grids, classes and mission/job/run/image/pipeline lineage. GeoTIFF preserves raster values; GeoJSON/Shapefile follow pixel boundaries, without inventing resolution. Shapefile ZIP includes SHP/SHX/DBF/PRJ/CPG and metadata. Download links appear on the completed execution. Outputs do not automatically become references.</p><h3>Controls and recovery</h3><p>Pause/Resume apply at supported safe checkpoints. Stop preserves completed artifacts; a stopped native run starts again as a new attempt. Delete archives configurations/runs and retains scientific data. Removing actual data is not implied. A qualified model returned to training cannot run; changed release IDs require new operational work. On restart, completed workflow blocks remain completed; unfinished native stages restart with new run IDs, preserving earlier attempts. A pipeline Stop disables its dispatch until explicitly activated again.</p><h3>Activity &amp; Logs</h3><p>Open Activity &amp; Logs from any mission workspace. Filter mission, severity, run, pipeline, model and time. Expand technical details or download .log/JSONL diagnostics. The actual file directory is shown. Operational events are kept for 30 days in SQLite, while qualification/control audit events are retained. JSONL files rotate at 2 MiB, with five backups. Keys and credentials are redacted; raster pixels are not logged.</p><h3>Extending mission types</h3><p>Add mission type registers a named category using installed adapter families. A new algorithm or detector still needs a declared worker, typed inputs/outputs, training support and qualification metrics. Detection is unavailable until a compatible detector is installed and qualified. The graph does not execute generated code.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'source-labels', group: 'Analysis', title: 'Start a classifier: pretrained weights or your labels',
      summary: 'Draw labels before the first run; import external polygons; understand what is pretrained.',
      keywords: 'labels annotation training pretrained weights source image rectangle polygon point shapefile pseudo labels SAM',
      html: `<h3>Which models can start without labels?</h3><p>Dynamic World has a pretrained nine-class head for Sentinel-2 L1C. Prithvi crop/LULC has a pretrained head for three real HLS dates. OpenEarthMap has a pretrained head for very high resolution RGB, not ordinary Sentinel/Landsat imagery. Each model must receive its required sensor, bands, resolution and units.</p><p>RF, SVM and XGBoost fit your classes from labeled examples. The generic U-Net starts from scratch or continues a compatible local checkpoint. Generic Prithvi has a frozen pretrained encoder but needs labels for its downstream head. The LULC Ensemble combines compatible fitted members.</p><h3>Prepare labels before a first run</h3><ol><li>Connect the image in Source data.</li><li>Open the visible Labels &amp; training tab, choose a job and image, then Open image.</li><li>Draw a rectangle, polygon (Finish polygon), or point. Scroll to zoom; select Pan to move.</li><li>Assign a known land-cover class, confidence and source note. Choose Training or Validation. Save reference.</li><li>Draw representative examples for at least two classes. Validation never enters fitting. Small regions may need a finer analysis grid.</li><li>Return to <b>Training &amp; qualification</b> to train a candidate, check independent validation and qualify it. Choose the qualified model in <b>Manual inference</b>.</li></ol><p>External labels: use Import external polygons, upload a zipped shapefile or matching SHP/SHX/DBF files, supply the actual CRS, map class attributes, choose the image and label role, then import. Imported classifications must match your intended taxonomy.</p><h3>Polygon export</h3><p>In Runs &amp; results, choose Export class polygons · GeoJSON. The background export traces four-connected regions from the original classification, including holes, and excludes nodata. GeoJSON uses WGS84 coordinates with class_id and class_name attributes and saved source/grid lineage. Boundaries follow pixel edges; no smoothing or extra resolution is invented. A size budget protects memory and may require a smaller AOI for exceptionally large outputs.</p><h3>Insights: large rasters and workspace</h3><p>Save existing job results into the library without reducing their original resolution. Validation scans blocks; the copy remains immutable. Before planning operations, use Processing grid below the chat to keep the native grid (up to 4 million pixels) or explicitly choose a smaller grid. The saved plan shows exact dimensions, CRS, pixel sizes, resampling and nodata rules. Approve only after reviewing these. Class/mask grids use nearest neighbor; continuous values use bilinear. Reduced results can omit small regions. Map previews are bounded display copies. Chat &amp; plans, Map and Side by side views provide separate workspaces. LLM connection opens a large settings dialog.</p><h3>Predictions and SAM</h3><p>Dynamic World outputs are model predictions. Review and correct Dynamic World predictions before using them as starting training labels. Keep independent validation references in a separate split. In Review on image, use the visible SAM + point, SAM − point or Draw SAM box toolbar buttons. Click directly on the image or drag a box, then select Segment points or Run SAM for box. Clear prompts removes the visible markers. SAM 3 Tracker proposes boundaries from a point or box; it does not assign semantic land-cover classes. Only an explicitly classed and human-approved proposal becomes a reference.</p><p>Saved annotations keep the source revision, original image identity, grid and geometry. Changing source data requires reopening the image. Original scientific pixels and prior outputs are preserved.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'glossary' , group: 'Reference', title: 'Glossary & current limitations',
      summary: 'The terms and boundaries used throughout the application.',
      keywords: 'glossary definitions limitation unsupported feature terminology MCP MC2 AOI LULC STAC taxonomy',
      html: `<dl><dt>AOI</dt><dd>Area of interest: your search or processing region.</dd><dt>LULC</dt><dd>Land use/land cover. Pixel classes use a particular taxonomy; visually similar labels from different models need not mean the same thing.</dd><dt>STAC catalog</dt><dd>Searchable geospatial metadata and asset references. It is not a guarantee of free raster download rights or availability.</dd><dt>MC² / MapControl</dt><dd>The embedded mapping service used for AOIs, footprints and result display. The application calls its MCP tools.</dd><dt>MCP</dt><dd>Model Context Protocol: the service interface used for map actions. It is not a LULC algorithm.</dd><dt>Raster / band / cube</dt><dd>A georeferenced pixel array; a band is one channel, and a cube contains several spatially aligned spectral channels.</dd><dt>Multispectral / hyperspectral</dt><dd>Multiple spectral bands / many narrower bands. A three-band preview does not represent the entire scientific cube.</dd><dt>CRS</dt><dd>Coordinate reference system, including geographic or projected coordinates. EPSG is an identifier scheme for many CRSs.</dd><dt>Coregistration</dt><dd>Spatial alignment. This app's alignment tools correct band translations within an image.</dd><dt>Inference</dt><dd>Applying a fitted model to produce predictions. Some job workflows fit a model before predicting.</dd><dt>Reference / ground truth</dt><dd>A human-approved label with geometry, scope and provenance. It can still be mistaken; automatic predictions are not ground truth.</dd><dt>Validation / test</dt><dd>References held out from fitting; validation can guide model selection, while untouched test data assesses the fixed selection.</dd><dt>Checkpoint / candidate</dt><dd>A saved model state / a new separately versioned training outcome.</dd><dt>Consensus / disagreement</dt><dd>A combination of predictions / where compared predictions differ. Neither is independent truth.</dd><dt>Percentile / vote</dt><dd>A score rank / a detector decision count. Neither is a calibrated probability.</dd></dl>
        <h3>Current product boundaries</h3><ul><li>Catalog and preview access are separate from science-raster analysis.</li><li>Local imagery imports connect to mission/job creation. Only installed adapters and supported vector formats can be used. Operational promotion requires a supported model and its qualification evidence.</li><li>Archived hyperspectral bundles are not automatically unpacked into calibrated stacks.</li><li>Native pretrained heads require their released sensor/product/band/taxonomy contracts and run separately.</li><li>Prithvi's original foundation worker freezes the encoder; SAM fine-tuning is unsupported.</li><li>Band alignment does not perform multi-scene or multi-date registration.</li><li>Review outputs are bounded-grid analyses, not guaranteed full-scene native-resolution products.</li><li>Automatic learning needs the backend running and sufficient new relevant evidence. Training references below confidence 3/5 are excluded by the job worker.</li><li>Metrics require the documented reference scope. Benchmark pilots and agreement figures are not proof of global accuracy.</li><li>Persistence is local; backups and deployment access controls remain operator responsibilities.</li></ul>`
    }
  ]
});
